WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:16.800
Tim Ferriss: Hello, boys and girls, ladies
and germs.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:18.030
This is Tim Ferriss.

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:20.610
Welcome to another episode of The Tim Ferriss
Show.

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:24.580
I'm going to hop straight into the guest because
I have so many questions I would like to ask.

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:28.599
My guest today is Jonathan Haidt, spelled
H-A-I-D-T.

00:00:28.599 --> 00:00:30.960
You can find him on Twitter @jonhaidt.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:35.140
He's a social psychologist at New York University's
Stern School of Business.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:39.160
Jonathan received his PhD from the University
of Pennsylvania in 1992.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:43.460
His research examines the intuitive foundations
of morality in how morality varies across

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:45.800
cultural and political divisions.

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:50.360
He is the author of The Happiness Hypothesis
and the New York Times bestsellers The Righteous

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:54.050
Mind and The Coddling of the American Mind
(with Greg Lukianoff).

00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:59.750
He has given four TED Talks and in 2019 he
was inducted into the American Academy of

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:01.210
Arts and Sciences.

00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:05.409
Since 2018, Jonathan has been studying the
contributions of social media to the decline

00:01:05.409 --> 00:01:08.420
of teen mental health and the rise of political
dysfunction.

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:13.689
He is currently writing two books: Kids in
Space: Why Teen Mental Health Is Collapsing

00:01:13.689 --> 00:01:17.150
and Life after Babel — great title, also.

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:20.860
Life after Babel: Adapting to a World We Can
No Longer Share.

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:26.250
You can find him at jonathanhaidt.com and
on Twitter once again @jonhaidt.

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:28.960
Jon, nice to see you.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:29.960
Thanks for making the time.

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:31.659
Jonathan Haidt: Oh, my pleasure, Tim.

00:01:31.659 --> 00:01:32.900
Very nice to meet you.

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:37.470
Tim Ferriss: I thought we would begin with
influences.

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:46.960
How your personality, how your thinking has
taken shape and I wanted to begin with the

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:49.320
name Richard Shweder.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:55.930
Could you please explain who Richard is and
how he factors into your life story?

00:01:55.930 --> 00:02:02.760
Jonathan Haidt: I have many mentors and sometimes
I do what I call the gratitude parade in my

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:06.890
head and people flash past and there's a string
of mentors.

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:13.249
Rick Shweder is the biggest on who I am, and
what I've written.

00:02:13.249 --> 00:02:18.290
Rick Shweder is a cultural anthropologist
at the University of Chicago and when I wrote

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:23.510
my dissertation at Penn, I wrote it on a debate
that he was having with some other people

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:29.040
who study morality who were saying that morality
is always about harm, rights, and justice.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:33.459
Shweder said, "Well yeah, where you live in
Cambridge, Massachusetts it is, but around

00:02:33.459 --> 00:02:37.489
the world the moral domain is much broader
and there's the ethics of community, ethics

00:02:37.489 --> 00:02:38.830
of divinity."

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:43.959
I planned a study to pit the two theories
against each other and I did a study in Brazil

00:02:43.959 --> 00:02:49.110
and the US across social classes and Shweder's
predictions really came out beautifully.

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:53.819
I found big cultural variation, especially
social class variation in morality.

00:02:53.819 --> 00:02:58.190
In part because of that I was able to get
a postdoc doing research with him.

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:02.780
For two years after my PhD, I spent at the
University of Chicago and during that time

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:07.680
I got a fellowship to go to India for three
months where Shweder worked in Bhubaneswar

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.440
in the state of Odisha, India.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:14.890
That period, that year, 1993, I hope that's
a year that we'll come back to a couple times

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:18.629
during this conversation because that is the
pivotal year in my life when my mind was blown

00:03:18.629 --> 00:03:20.040
in many ways.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:24.830
One of which was that I was reading Buddhism
and Hinduism and then I spent three months

00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:30.970
in India and it really was welcome to my world
and my way of thinking because Shweder is

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:39.510
the most brilliant, iconoclastic, thinking
for himself, perverse in the most wonderful

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:42.150
way, intellectual I've ever met.

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:44.080
That's the beginning of who Rick Shweder is.

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:45.640
Tim Ferriss: Perverse intellectuals.

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:49.989
That might be the headline of this episode.

00:03:49.989 --> 00:03:51.830
We are going to come back to 1993.

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:54.310
We're definitely going to come back to Bhubaneswar.

00:03:54.310 --> 00:03:55.799
I was wondering how to pronounce that.

00:03:55.799 --> 00:03:59.209
I'm glad you gave me the layup in advance.

00:03:59.209 --> 00:04:02.260
I'd like to visit a motto, I believe.

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:07.980
You can't believe everything you read on the
internet, but hopefully The Atlantic is reliable

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:09.379
in this respect.

00:04:09.379 --> 00:04:15.970
A motto of Rick's that I'd like to unpack,
which is, "If someone asserts it, try denying

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:17.400
it and see if that makes sense.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:20.940
If someone denies it, try asserting it and
see if that makes sense."

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:21.940
Jonathan Haidt: Yes.

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:23.810
Tim Ferriss: Could you explain what this means?

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:24.810
Jonathan Haidt: Yes.

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:29.370
Tim Ferriss: And maybe give an example of
how he used it or would encourage students

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:30.370
to use it.

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:36.460
Jonathan Haidt: It comes from, I think a brother-in-law
of Rick's who is a dermatologist.

00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:40.280
The dermatologist once said to him, "Oh, dermatology
is easy.

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:41.280
Just follow this rule.

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:42.380
If it's dry, make it wet.

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:44.810
If it's wet, make it dry."

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:50.259
Rick being, again, this perversity, he takes
that rule and that was sort of the way he

00:04:50.259 --> 00:04:52.080
ran his intellectual life.

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:59.380
When I went to Chicago, it's like the most
intense intellectual square mile in the country.

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:04.071
You'd be in these seminars with all these
Nobel Prize-winning economists and people

00:05:04.071 --> 00:05:09.590
and someone would say something and Rick would
turn it around and say, "Is the opposite true?"

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:13.250
Then he would ask, "Well isn't also the opposite
of what you're saying true?"

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:19.120
Then you'd have to think, wait, actually,
yeah, that is too, sometimes.

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:27.720
What I take from it is we have such pressures
to fall into line with our team to go with

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:30.160
what the dominant view is.

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:33.880
If you do that, you're going to end up being
kind of a hack.

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:37.130
You're going to end up being like everyone
else.

00:05:37.130 --> 00:05:42.050
If you just automatically question things,
not to be a jerk, now you could do it and

00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:43.050
be a jerk.

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:47.960
If you just question it just with pure intellectual
curiosity, is it true if we turn it around?

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:50.160
Usually it's not, but often it is.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:53.440
That's how you get new insights.

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:59.780
If you just have that as a habit, yeah, this
is definitely good advice for anyone in a

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:02.960
creative industry or in the academic world.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:04.630
Just try turning things around.

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:12.419
Tim Ferriss: How would, just as an example,
Rick potentially use this in anthropology?

00:06:12.419 --> 00:06:19.710
It might have been a broader guiding tenet
of his thinking that applied to much more

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:25.370
outside of his sort of declared field of expertise.

00:06:25.370 --> 00:06:31.100
Is there an example you might give for how
that would fit into anthropology or the type

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:33.790
of research that you were doing at the time?

00:06:33.790 --> 00:06:37.720
Jonathan Haidt: So one pops to mind, but it's
so offensive that I have to start with something

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:39.900
else, because otherwise I'll lose your listeners.

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:42.410
Tim Ferriss: You have to warm up the listening
hamstrings.

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:48.210
Jonathan Haidt: Just one example is he was
in a discussion with some psychologist because

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:51.150
he's an anthropologist but he's in cultural
psychology.

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:52.169
I'm a psychologist.

00:06:52.169 --> 00:06:56.729
He's in a discussion with some psychologists
and they were saying, "Well, I mean, we have

00:06:56.729 --> 00:07:00.730
experimental evidence on this and that's just
so much more reliable than ethnography.

00:07:00.730 --> 00:07:05.400
I mean ethnography, an anthropologist goes
someplace and they take notes and they report,

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:08.090
but that's just one person's view.

00:07:08.090 --> 00:07:09.840
Experimental evidence is just much more reliable."

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:13.660
He turns around and he says, "Oh, really?

00:07:13.660 --> 00:07:15.080
Well, let's see.

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:18.330
Then you look at experiments that often don't
replicate.

00:07:18.330 --> 00:07:22.800
Then you look at anthropological accounts
where often multiple anthropologists will

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:26.640
go someplace and they will find the same thing."

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:31.530
The point is just you automatically assume,
well, of course experiments with numbers and

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:33.530
careful procedures are better.

00:07:33.530 --> 00:07:36.690
He just sort of turns it around and says,
"Well, actually, maybe not."

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:37.690
Okay.

00:07:37.690 --> 00:07:38.690
That's the easy one.

00:07:38.690 --> 00:07:39.970
I hope listeners are with me on that.

00:07:39.970 --> 00:07:49.000
Here's the hard one, just about the most offensive
cultural practice in the world for anthropologists

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:52.210
and enlightened people is called FGM.

00:07:52.210 --> 00:08:00.270
Now that stands for female genital mutilation
and anthropologists and Progressives are so

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:04.840
tolerant, so quick to say, "Let's not condemn
other cultures.

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:06.110
Let's look at it from their point of view."

00:08:06.110 --> 00:08:08.180
On this issue they don't.

00:08:08.180 --> 00:08:13.789
What Rick was doing was saying, "In the societies
where they do this, let's try to understand

00:08:13.789 --> 00:08:15.069
what they're doing."

00:08:15.069 --> 00:08:22.130
He's totally open to the fact that sometimes
it really is a horrible operation that deprives

00:08:22.130 --> 00:08:24.479
the woman of sexuality.

00:08:24.479 --> 00:08:30.870
There are such a range of practices, but if
you simply say any modification on a female

00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:37.709
should be punishable by time in jail, well
then what happens is you have a variety of

00:08:37.709 --> 00:08:41.709
African and Islamic immigrant groups in the
US and Canada where the mothers are put in

00:08:41.709 --> 00:08:42.779
jail.

00:08:42.779 --> 00:08:46.410
Rick says,"Well, let's question this.

00:08:46.410 --> 00:08:51.150
Once you see what they're doing, and sometimes
it's much more equivalent to male circumcision,

00:08:51.150 --> 00:08:53.310
which is common around the world."

00:08:53.310 --> 00:08:59.269
Anyway, actually, no, your listeners probably
aren't going to bolt just because of that.

00:08:59.269 --> 00:09:02.260
I might get in trouble in other parts of the
academic world.

00:09:02.260 --> 00:09:07.311
Tim Ferriss: If they bolt just because of
that, then honestly this is culling of the

00:09:07.311 --> 00:09:11.160
herd because let me, before you continue,
please hold your place, bookmark.

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:17.840
Folks, part of the reason I wanted to have
Jon on, and I respect the work that he does,

00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:28.589
is that I believe using this type of examination
and self-examination, you become more antifragile

00:09:28.589 --> 00:09:34.790
and you can also see through the veil of your
own conditioning in a more effective way.

00:09:34.790 --> 00:09:41.360
You can examine your thinking and your assumptions
vis-à-vis the language you use, the language

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:45.459
you've absorbed, the labels that are your
defaults, et cetera.

00:09:45.459 --> 00:09:50.450
I think this makes you a better, more effective,
and hopefully, ultimately, happier person

00:09:50.450 --> 00:09:52.660
who's a better contributor to society.

00:09:52.660 --> 00:09:53.660
There's my sales pitch.

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:54.870
Jonathan Haidt: Wow, perfect.

00:09:54.870 --> 00:09:56.120
We're done here.

00:09:56.120 --> 00:09:57.779
You said it.

00:09:57.779 --> 00:09:59.430
That's exactly right.

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:01.360
That's why I'm so grateful to Rick.

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:05.810
Because if you develop this as a habit, you
become more intellectually antifragile, which

00:10:05.810 --> 00:10:06.810
we'll get to.

00:10:06.810 --> 00:10:09.130
I hope it helps you step out of your team.

00:10:09.130 --> 00:10:14.050
It helps you think independently and you can
see that your team does a lot of stupid things.

00:10:14.050 --> 00:10:15.649
Every team does.

00:10:15.649 --> 00:10:17.100
Yes, what you said.

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:19.800
Tim Ferriss: What I said.

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:20.879
All right.

00:10:20.879 --> 00:10:28.810
Then we flash back to 1993, and I would love
to revisit, I'm going to write this down sort

00:10:28.810 --> 00:10:38.350
of moral relativism and how we might think
about that and how we either embrace that

00:10:38.350 --> 00:10:41.380
or develop an awareness around it.

00:10:41.380 --> 00:10:46.020
Let's postpone 1993 and actually just tackle
this right now.

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:50.980
There are a number of books I read a long
time ago when I was part of something called

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:53.300
the Henry Crown Fellowship at the Aspen Institute.

00:10:53.300 --> 00:11:01.630
One, I am, I want to say it was, I want to
say H.G. Wells (George Orwell, Ed.), but I

00:11:01.630 --> 00:11:06.400
don't think that is right, who wrote an essay
called "Shooting [an] Elephant" or something

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:08.070
along these lines.

00:11:08.070 --> 00:11:12.580
You may actually be able to track back the
proper author of this, but it spoke of sort

00:11:12.580 --> 00:11:21.090
of social pressures and during British colonialism,
this series of events that led to someone

00:11:21.090 --> 00:11:25.620
feeling like he needed to shoot an elephant
that was causing all this ruckus in a village

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:27.440
even though he didn't want to.

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:34.740
That was describing social pressures and how
those can lead to actions that we find immoral

00:11:34.740 --> 00:11:36.810
or reprehensible ourselves.

00:11:36.810 --> 00:11:39.209
Putting that aside, that's just an example.

00:11:39.209 --> 00:11:48.930
We also read a lot about morality, not in
a structured way, I think, that would maybe

00:11:48.930 --> 00:11:52.180
stand toe to toe with the type of research
you do in any way.

00:11:52.180 --> 00:11:57.730
But reading various accounts of people succumbing
to moral relativism where everything is okay

00:11:57.730 --> 00:12:01.050
because it's in a different culture, it's
in a different time.

00:12:01.050 --> 00:12:02.850
How would you suggest people think about that?

00:12:02.850 --> 00:12:06.560
Because on one hand, yeah, I think that's
dangerous.

00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:12.431
On the other hand there's, there's so much
intolerance, masquerading as tolerance and

00:12:12.431 --> 00:12:14.959
there's so many issues that I'm sure we'll
get into.

00:12:14.959 --> 00:12:21.870
Just with the subject of moral relativism,
how do you think about that?

00:12:21.870 --> 00:12:26.510
Jonathan Haidt: This is a great topic to tackle
in a longer discussion.

00:12:26.510 --> 00:12:32.079
Because it's one of the oldest questions in
philosophy, it's an important one and it takes

00:12:32.079 --> 00:12:34.480
a little doing.

00:12:34.480 --> 00:12:39.880
There are a lot of intellectual puzzles where
we just naturally think about it on a single

00:12:39.880 --> 00:12:42.240
dimension or as a binary.

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:46.699
If you kind of break it up into multiple dimensions,
then you see solutions.

00:12:46.699 --> 00:12:50.880
The typical way of thinking about this is
there is morality real?

00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:58.550
Is it a real thing, the laws of physics such
that if people are behaving contrary to it,

00:12:58.550 --> 00:13:00.880
they're wrong even if they don't know it.

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:08.850
Or is morality just Herodotus and ancient
Greeks, they saw this, "Well, in this country

00:13:08.850 --> 00:13:13.209
they kill their parents when they get old,
and in this country they eat pigs," and they

00:13:13.209 --> 00:13:15.260
could see that practices differed.

00:13:15.260 --> 00:13:20.310
Well maybe it's just like language, it's just
like whatever happens in your culture, well

00:13:20.310 --> 00:13:23.000
that's what's grammatical.

00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:26.570
What I learned in part from Shweder and also
reading a lot of philosophy, some of which

00:13:26.570 --> 00:13:29.740
he guided me to, was I'll put out a couple
of concepts.

00:13:29.740 --> 00:13:35.769
The first is moral monism versus moral pluralism.

00:13:35.769 --> 00:13:39.040
The question isn't, "Is there moral truth
or not?"

00:13:39.040 --> 00:13:42.370
It's, "Is there one moral truth or multiple?"

00:13:42.370 --> 00:13:45.410
Once you do that, now you've loosened things
up.

00:13:45.410 --> 00:13:49.570
Because a hallmark of my work, and this is
where I departed from Rick a bit, is I've

00:13:49.570 --> 00:13:54.200
always been very influenced by evolutionary
psychology as well as anthropology.

00:13:54.200 --> 00:14:00.220
We are evolved creatures, we are homo sapiens
and we are all homo sapiens and we are primates

00:14:00.220 --> 00:14:03.880
and we have a lot in common with chimpanzees
and bonobos.

00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:05.940
That's the evolutionary side.

00:14:05.940 --> 00:14:08.839
Then there's also the anthropology, we vary.

00:14:08.839 --> 00:14:13.080
When you look around the world, you don't
find infinite variation, you find a few major

00:14:13.080 --> 00:14:14.230
patterns.

00:14:14.230 --> 00:14:18.790
A wonderful book, Dancing in the Streets:
A History of Collective Joy by Barbara Ehrenreich.

00:14:18.790 --> 00:14:24.430
She says, when the European explorers went
out and the 16th, 17th century, they found

00:14:24.430 --> 00:14:28.870
wherever they went, people danced around campfires,
had rituals that bonded the group together,

00:14:28.870 --> 00:14:31.880
they often used psychedelic substances, they
had beat heavy music.

00:14:31.880 --> 00:14:35.589
This is human nature, but feudal systems are
also pretty common.

00:14:35.589 --> 00:14:38.600
There are a few major patterns.

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:43.480
One possibility is there's not just one way
of doing things, but it's not anything goes.

00:14:43.480 --> 00:14:47.670
It's that there are several different sort
of grammatical human forms.

00:14:47.670 --> 00:14:52.890
This actually became the basis of my major
work in psychology, which is moral foundation's

00:14:52.890 --> 00:14:59.180
theory, which is my effort to say what are
the taste buds of the moral sense?

00:14:59.180 --> 00:15:01.399
Because cuisine varies around the world.

00:15:01.399 --> 00:15:05.769
You wouldn't say that the French are wrong
if they don't cook like the Vietnamese.

00:15:05.769 --> 00:15:10.070
I think you could say that the French and
the Vietnamese are both much better cooks

00:15:10.070 --> 00:15:11.670
than the English, or at least they were historically.

00:15:11.670 --> 00:15:13.579
There is some truth.

00:15:13.579 --> 00:15:17.259
It's not that anything goes, there is some
truth.

00:15:17.259 --> 00:15:20.891
Here's where we're bringing the second kind
of abstract but really powerful concept, which

00:15:20.891 --> 00:15:24.460
is anthropocentric truth.

00:15:24.460 --> 00:15:31.790
That means something can be true relative
to humans, to who we are.

00:15:31.790 --> 00:15:36.010
If intelligent aliens come to our solar system,
they will find that the Earth is the third

00:15:36.010 --> 00:15:37.080
planet from the Sun.

00:15:37.080 --> 00:15:39.880
That was true before we existed, and it'll
be true long after we're gone.

00:15:39.880 --> 00:15:43.500
They would find that gold is a better conductor
of electricity than aluminum.

00:15:43.500 --> 00:15:44.550
That was true before we were here.

00:15:44.550 --> 00:15:46.019
It'll be true.

00:15:46.019 --> 00:15:47.670
Those are non-anthropocentric truths.

00:15:47.670 --> 00:15:53.690
There's a fact and many people have been on
a quest to find what are the moral truths.

00:15:53.690 --> 00:15:58.519
Sam Harris, who for a brief time was my intellectual
enemy, we kind of had a feud for a few years.

00:15:58.519 --> 00:16:01.740
Now we're friends, we actually like each other
quite a lot.

00:16:01.740 --> 00:16:05.170
We had kind of a feud over this because he
has a whole book about the landscape of morality

00:16:05.170 --> 00:16:08.180
arguing that moral truths are like the truths
of chemistry.

00:16:08.180 --> 00:16:09.410
I say no.

00:16:09.410 --> 00:16:10.630
They are anthropocentric truths.

00:16:10.630 --> 00:16:14.180
This is from a philosopher David Wiggins.

00:16:14.180 --> 00:16:18.180
There are things that are true because of
the creatures we are.

00:16:18.180 --> 00:16:21.440
There are things that are beautiful because
of our senses.

00:16:21.440 --> 00:16:26.870
There are things that are, so Shakespeare
is a better writer than Kurt Vonnegut.

00:16:26.870 --> 00:16:31.860
Now I love reading Kurt Vonnegut, I probably
enjoy it more than Shakespeare, but man, I

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:36.519
can see and almost everyone can, Shakespeare
is a complete genius at crafting words.

00:16:36.519 --> 00:16:37.920
Now that's a fact.

00:16:37.920 --> 00:16:41.180
If aliens come here from another planet, they're
not going to agree because it doesn't make

00:16:41.180 --> 00:16:42.180
sense.

00:16:42.180 --> 00:16:43.180
It wouldn't fit with their nature.

00:16:43.180 --> 00:16:48.930
Anyway, so putting this all together, I think
what we can say is there are moral truths

00:16:48.930 --> 00:16:50.500
and moral orders that are emergent.

00:16:50.500 --> 00:16:54.519
I am not a relativist, I'm not a monist, I
am a moral pluralist.

00:16:54.519 --> 00:16:56.810
There are multiple moral truths.

00:16:56.810 --> 00:17:02.459
These truths are emergent from human nature
interacting as people interact in large in

00:17:02.459 --> 00:17:03.459
societies.

00:17:03.459 --> 00:17:04.459
Okay, wow.

00:17:04.459 --> 00:17:05.459
I think I just did it.

00:17:05.459 --> 00:17:06.459
Tim Ferriss: It was great.

00:17:06.459 --> 00:17:11.610
I want to tag on a follow up, which is how
do you personally, and we're going to come

00:17:11.610 --> 00:17:17.760
back to from feud to friends story because
I think that could be an instructive case

00:17:17.760 --> 00:17:22.520
study plus, I'm just curious because I'm friends
with Sam also, who is a great, he's a very

00:17:22.520 --> 00:17:24.070
formidable sparring partner.

00:17:24.070 --> 00:17:25.730
Jonathan Haidt: Yes.

00:17:25.730 --> 00:17:36.640
Tim Ferriss: When you look at global events,
how do you think about, personally, human

00:17:36.640 --> 00:17:48.890
rights violations, what to accept, what to
oppose yourself as a pluralist?

00:17:48.890 --> 00:17:52.560
I'm not saying that because I'm viewing myself
as the opposite of a pluralist.

00:17:52.560 --> 00:17:59.260
I'm just curious how you personally think
about what you are proactively going to push

00:17:59.260 --> 00:18:01.480
back against.

00:18:01.480 --> 00:18:02.750
Doesn't need to be outside the US.

00:18:02.750 --> 00:18:07.789
Jonathan Haidt: Because I'm an emergentist,
that is I believe that morals emerge as we

00:18:07.789 --> 00:18:08.789
interact.

00:18:08.789 --> 00:18:14.120
I don't think that 50,000 years ago, societies
were wrong in dividing labor between male

00:18:14.120 --> 00:18:15.350
and female.

00:18:15.350 --> 00:18:18.030
It's not like they were wrong and they should
have fixed it.

00:18:18.030 --> 00:18:19.990
They should have had perfect — no.

00:18:19.990 --> 00:18:24.620
As we now have modern societies with labor-saving
devices and you don't have to divide labor

00:18:24.620 --> 00:18:27.540
between male and female, and there are other
ways to live.

00:18:27.540 --> 00:18:32.280
Now any society that was to say, "Well, women
can't do certain things, only men can."

00:18:32.280 --> 00:18:34.610
Well that would be wrong.

00:18:34.610 --> 00:18:36.340
In a modern Western society, that would be
unethical.

00:18:36.340 --> 00:18:39.230
That would be a violation of civil rights.

00:18:39.230 --> 00:18:43.250
Now when we look at things like countries
in which the secret police grab people in

00:18:43.250 --> 00:18:46.750
the night and take them away and torture and
kill them, you don't have to be a very advanced

00:18:46.750 --> 00:18:51.400
society to realize that this is horrific,
this is wrong, this is brutality.

00:18:51.400 --> 00:18:53.940
This is not justified by appeals to morals.

00:18:53.940 --> 00:18:55.750
I once developed a kind of a three-part test.

00:18:55.750 --> 00:18:57.789
Let me see if I can remember it.

00:18:57.789 --> 00:19:02.750
Because what we want to do is we want to separate
things like veiling or things where they might

00:19:02.750 --> 00:19:08.340
be broadly accepted in a culture, but we are
offended by them from things like chattel

00:19:08.340 --> 00:19:13.640
slavery where some people accept it but the
victims of it didn't accept it.

00:19:13.640 --> 00:19:17.591
I think we can point to things, it's often
tricky, but we can point to things where we

00:19:17.591 --> 00:19:21.311
can say, we can criticize that practice and
that culture.

00:19:21.311 --> 00:19:25.809
In extreme cases we might even say we should
try to undermine it or try to stop it.

00:19:25.809 --> 00:19:28.520
We'd have to go case by case.

00:19:28.520 --> 00:19:33.600
Widow burning in India and things like that,
which I think will clearly fall on one side.

00:19:33.600 --> 00:19:36.030
That's how I would generally do it in the
abstract.

00:19:36.030 --> 00:19:40.169
Now I think you asked me a slightly different
question, which was more like me personally,

00:19:40.169 --> 00:19:41.169
not philosophically.

00:19:41.169 --> 00:19:42.169
Right?

00:19:42.169 --> 00:19:43.169
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:19:43.169 --> 00:19:47.220
Jonathan Haidt: A funny thing happened to
me as I've studied morality, which is that

00:19:47.220 --> 00:19:49.660
I almost never get angry anymore.

00:19:49.660 --> 00:19:56.930
Before 1993, I was a normal person who got
angry a lot and I'm pretty even-tempered.

00:19:56.930 --> 00:19:58.880
It wasn't like I had anger problems.

00:19:58.880 --> 00:20:05.809
I'd read the newspaper, I'd be angry at the
goddamn Ronald Reagan or whatever.

00:20:05.809 --> 00:20:08.330
Or someone cuts me off or something's slow.

00:20:08.330 --> 00:20:15.380
I was sort of quasi type A, this "pushy, smart
alec son of a bitch Jewish kid" is what my

00:20:15.380 --> 00:20:17.130
neighbor once called me.

00:20:17.130 --> 00:20:18.640
I used to be like that.

00:20:18.640 --> 00:20:25.919
Then in 1993 I changed in part from, the key
thing there was actually taking LSD for the

00:20:25.919 --> 00:20:31.760
first time while I was studying to go to India
and reading the Bhagavad Gita and reading

00:20:31.760 --> 00:20:35.409
Hinduism and Hindu philosophy.

00:20:35.409 --> 00:20:37.110
I was at 29 years old.

00:20:37.110 --> 00:20:44.280
Here I am studying moral psychology and all
these things and in that year I kind of stepped

00:20:44.280 --> 00:20:45.320
out of the matrix.

00:20:45.320 --> 00:20:51.020
I kind of stepped out of the world that I
had been living in.

00:20:51.020 --> 00:20:55.630
That is what allowed me, that year allowed
me to do the work I've done with the rest

00:20:55.630 --> 00:20:56.630
of my life.

00:20:56.630 --> 00:20:57.630
Which is saying, "You know what?

00:20:57.630 --> 00:21:01.690
Let's try to understand Progressives and Conservatives
in their own terms."

00:21:01.690 --> 00:21:07.820
Before then, I was just a standard, straight-issue,
Ivy League, Liberal, Progressive type person.

00:21:07.820 --> 00:21:13.960
A roundabout way of answering your question
is I don't get mad.

00:21:13.960 --> 00:21:18.560
I look at systems and I always think, "How
can we make them better?"

00:21:18.560 --> 00:21:23.330
Systems that are producing terrible results
for human beings should be changed.

00:21:23.330 --> 00:21:26.520
Actually I'll share, so one thing, this was
actually before my turning point.

00:21:26.520 --> 00:21:31.820
In 1987, just before grad school, I traveled
alone across Eastern Europe.

00:21:31.820 --> 00:21:36.100
That's why I went to Hungary, Romania, Bulgaria.

00:21:36.100 --> 00:21:42.160
When I was in Romania where Ceaușescu was
the dictator, the people were literally cold

00:21:42.160 --> 00:21:43.160
and dirty.

00:21:43.160 --> 00:21:46.860
They literally did not have soap to clean
their clothes.

00:21:46.860 --> 00:21:49.570
They did not have heat in the winter.

00:21:49.570 --> 00:21:53.179
They were kept like animals and they behaved
like animals in some sense.

00:21:53.179 --> 00:21:57.610
We try to get on a bus, there's so few buses,
people push and shove.

00:21:57.610 --> 00:21:58.640
It didn't make me angry.

00:21:58.640 --> 00:22:01.090
I was like, "Oh, my God."

00:22:01.090 --> 00:22:03.620
Actually it did make me angry.

00:22:03.620 --> 00:22:05.230
All of my feelings were against Ceaușescu.

00:22:05.230 --> 00:22:09.640
I thought, "If I could kill this man with
my bare hands right now, I would do it and

00:22:09.640 --> 00:22:10.640
I would love it."

00:22:10.640 --> 00:22:15.250
It was so horrific to see what this man had
done to this society, him and his half million

00:22:15.250 --> 00:22:17.360
secret policemen.

00:22:17.360 --> 00:22:23.740
When he was shot in the mud in a courtyard
in 1989, I was thrilled.

00:22:23.740 --> 00:22:25.490
I guess that's more of a direct answer to
your question.

00:22:25.490 --> 00:22:32.340
If I see someone oppressing a whole society
or acting in that monstrous way, not for any

00:22:32.340 --> 00:22:36.510
moral, morally legitimate reason, I think
we need to take action.

00:22:36.510 --> 00:22:41.990
Tim Ferriss: Could you tell me more about
your LSD experience?

00:22:41.990 --> 00:22:50.650
Why was that such a seemingly crucial ingredient
in that turning point or that period for you?

00:22:50.650 --> 00:22:54.000
Jonathan Haidt: Yeah, because I know you've
had a fair amount of psychedelic coverage

00:22:54.000 --> 00:22:58.320
on this podcast and I'm sure your listeners
will, many will be experienced or familiar

00:22:58.320 --> 00:23:00.410
with it.

00:23:00.410 --> 00:23:09.919
The turning point for me, it was June 11th,
1993, many other such experiences, one feels

00:23:09.919 --> 00:23:13.850
reborn and you feel like you remember the
day, you remember exactly when it happened.

00:23:13.850 --> 00:23:16.230
I was a postdoc in Chicago working with Shweder.

00:23:16.230 --> 00:23:21.520
I went back to Philadelphia where I'd been
a grad student and where my ex-girlfriend,

00:23:21.520 --> 00:23:27.610
we were still good friends, was there and
a lot of our friends and she got some acid

00:23:27.610 --> 00:23:28.610
and we all did it.

00:23:28.610 --> 00:23:32.130
It was my first time doing it at the age of
29.

00:23:32.130 --> 00:23:38.700
The metaphor that I use in making sense of
it afterwards was it felt to me as though

00:23:38.700 --> 00:23:45.000
I'd spent all my life living in this amazing
mansion with thousands of rooms.

00:23:45.000 --> 00:23:49.990
I loved exploring this mansion and I loved
to travel and there were all these rooms to

00:23:49.990 --> 00:23:55.120
explore and I thought I was seeing all this
stuff.

00:23:55.120 --> 00:23:59.670
Then that night it was like God punched a
hole in the roof, reached in, picked me up,

00:23:59.670 --> 00:24:03.590
took me out of the mansion, and took me around
the whole world and said, "You thought that

00:24:03.590 --> 00:24:04.650
was the world?

00:24:04.650 --> 00:24:07.289
No, this is the world.

00:24:07.289 --> 00:24:10.520
It's beyond anything you can imagine."

00:24:10.520 --> 00:24:18.169
Then he puts me back in the mansion, seals
up the roof and says, "Go on."

00:24:18.169 --> 00:24:21.110
I don't mean literally God, I'm a Jewish atheist.

00:24:21.110 --> 00:24:23.090
I don't believe literally in God.

00:24:23.090 --> 00:24:30.529
These experiences have really helped me understand
the way we evolve from religion, the way almost

00:24:30.529 --> 00:24:35.870
all societies have a way of using psychedelic
plants.

00:24:35.870 --> 00:24:41.240
Oh, you interviewed Michael Pollan, so you
know what he said.

00:24:41.240 --> 00:24:44.990
That experience certainly changed me as a
psychologist thinking about the human mind

00:24:44.990 --> 00:24:47.640
and consciousness and self-transcendence and
all that.

00:24:47.640 --> 00:24:56.350
It changed me as a human being because afterwards
it was like small things just don't matter.

00:24:56.350 --> 00:25:00.060
It changes your perspective.

00:25:00.060 --> 00:25:02.340
That's trite to say.

00:25:02.340 --> 00:25:10.100
It also just helped me — what it did was
here at the same time I was reading the Bhagavad

00:25:10.100 --> 00:25:14.039
Gita and in the Bhagavad Gita, there is a
specific scene, I talk about this in The Righteous

00:25:14.039 --> 00:25:15.350
Mind or The Happiness Hypothesis.

00:25:15.350 --> 00:25:19.710
There's a specific scene where Krishna gives
Arjuna a third eye to see the world as it

00:25:19.710 --> 00:25:20.710
really is.

00:25:20.710 --> 00:25:22.270
He's like gasping for breath.

00:25:22.270 --> 00:25:26.720
He's like, he can't process it, but he comes
back and he wants to serve Krishna.

00:25:26.720 --> 00:25:32.030
Now I didn't come back, I want to serve my
fellow man, but I felt like all my old pettiness,

00:25:32.030 --> 00:25:35.800
all of that just sort of burned off.

00:25:35.800 --> 00:25:37.970
I don't know, I just had a kind of an independence.

00:25:37.970 --> 00:25:41.600
I'm not on a team, I just want to understand
what's happening.

00:25:41.600 --> 00:25:51.320
Tim Ferriss: Did that then provide the fertile
ground that allowed you to learn?

00:25:51.320 --> 00:25:57.990
I'm once again quoting from the Atlantic article,
the power of rituals and viewing a commitment

00:25:57.990 --> 00:26:00.270
to religious purity as a way to knit communities
together.

00:26:00.270 --> 00:26:05.650
In other words, without that experience, without
being plucked out of the mansion, do you think

00:26:05.650 --> 00:26:12.620
you would've come to those conclusions on
your own in India, or were they mutually dependent

00:26:12.620 --> 00:26:13.620
in a way?

00:26:13.620 --> 00:26:15.539
Jonathan Haidt: I think they were mutually
dependent.

00:26:15.539 --> 00:26:16.539
Yes.

00:26:16.539 --> 00:26:17.659
I don't think I could have done it.

00:26:17.659 --> 00:26:21.179
It would've been just more of a standard intellectual
experience.

00:26:21.179 --> 00:26:22.240
This is interesting.

00:26:22.240 --> 00:26:23.240
Let me write this down in my notebook.

00:26:23.240 --> 00:26:25.299
Let me try to make sense of this.

00:26:25.299 --> 00:26:30.280
Because the other piece we want to bring in
here is that in grad school I read Émile

00:26:30.280 --> 00:26:34.690
Durkheim, the French sociologist and that
so, and really comes out in The Righteous

00:26:34.690 --> 00:26:38.380
Mind like Rick Shweder and Émile Durkheim
and Charles Darwin.

00:26:38.380 --> 00:26:39.940
Those are the three heroes of the book.

00:26:39.940 --> 00:26:43.940
Those three men, their ideas just transform
my thinking.

00:26:43.940 --> 00:26:49.820
So you put those all together in a 29-year-old
brain who's having a tremendously good time

00:26:49.820 --> 00:26:54.279
at the University of Chicago and then going
to India and then as you're shaking it up,

00:26:54.279 --> 00:26:58.730
you put in just a tiny little bit of LSD,
and boom, it comes out into a totally new

00:26:58.730 --> 00:26:59.730
configuration.

00:26:59.730 --> 00:27:00.730
That's kind of what happened to me in 1993.

00:27:00.730 --> 00:27:08.070
Tim Ferriss: It is like the MSG of spiritual
multi-dimensional, just a little bit of pixie

00:27:08.070 --> 00:27:09.070
dust of LSD.

00:27:09.070 --> 00:27:10.760
Jonathan Haidt: Pixie dust, yes.

00:27:10.760 --> 00:27:11.760
Yeah.

00:27:11.760 --> 00:27:16.380
Well, you know, if you think about your frontal
cortex, it kind of locks down between 13 and

00:27:16.380 --> 00:27:17.860
sort of mid to late twenties.

00:27:17.860 --> 00:27:19.860
It's kind of locking down.

00:27:19.860 --> 00:27:25.150
It's very open to change in your teen years,
but it kind of settles in by your late twenties.

00:27:25.150 --> 00:27:29.391
As it's kind of setting that period of change,
had it been five or 10 years later, it might

00:27:29.391 --> 00:27:30.391
not have had that much effect.

00:27:30.391 --> 00:27:35.539
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, that would strike me as
a sort of critical formative window.

00:27:35.539 --> 00:27:42.620
If you are able to slow the setting of the
concrete, or something along those lines,

00:27:42.620 --> 00:27:45.030
metaphorically, then interesting things can
happen.

00:27:45.030 --> 00:27:52.279
I will say, not to spend too much time on
the psychedelic component of this, but that

00:27:52.279 --> 00:28:00.430
the loosening of self-identity, even at advanced
ages, let's just say in terminal cancer patients

00:28:00.430 --> 00:28:03.340
are suffering from end-of-life anxiety.

00:28:03.340 --> 00:28:10.110
The ability, and there's some evidence to
suggest, although I think it's simplistic,

00:28:10.110 --> 00:28:17.000
that the downregulation of the default mode
network helps to inhibit self-referential

00:28:17.000 --> 00:28:20.159
thinking and that type of executive function.

00:28:20.159 --> 00:28:24.279
Therefore people experience this ego dissolution
or ego death.

00:28:24.279 --> 00:28:35.470
Even in a less extreme instance of that, it
frees you from the constructs that have been

00:28:35.470 --> 00:28:39.870
the lenses you've always worn but not realized
you've been wearing.

00:28:39.870 --> 00:28:45.840
That's right to that extent, I do think it
allows you to see something like, for instance,

00:28:45.840 --> 00:28:53.940
these behaviors and beliefs in India with
a very different set of eyes than would otherwise

00:28:53.940 --> 00:28:57.000
be possible.

00:28:57.000 --> 00:29:03.770
1993, I must say, just as coincidence, 1992
to 1993 was also the year that probably most

00:29:03.770 --> 00:29:05.370
dramatically changed my life.

00:29:05.370 --> 00:29:09.320
We were having, so how's that transformative
experiences in the same 12-month period.

00:29:09.320 --> 00:29:10.320
Jonathan Haidt: The same time.

00:29:10.320 --> 00:29:18.710
Tim Ferriss: It was my first time overseas
and I landed in Japan as an exchange student

00:29:18.710 --> 00:29:23.960
going to a Japanese school living with Japanese
families for a year.

00:29:23.960 --> 00:29:34.940
That broke open my mind in such an incredibly,
at points difficult ways, but in such a beautifully

00:29:34.940 --> 00:29:42.990
profound and productive way because it forced
me to question what it meant to do things

00:29:42.990 --> 00:29:43.990
the right way.

00:29:43.990 --> 00:29:47.120
It was my first time being in a country where
people drove on the opposite side of the street.

00:29:47.120 --> 00:29:52.720
It was my first time being anywhere where
the entire family shared the bath, didn't

00:29:52.720 --> 00:29:57.820
change the water, and you had to clean yourself
beforehand.

00:29:57.820 --> 00:30:03.500
The examples just where they cool their food
by slurping it in with great noise as opposed

00:30:03.500 --> 00:30:06.360
to blowing on it with exhales.

00:30:06.360 --> 00:30:11.570
It was like this Alice in Wonderland, topsy-turvy
upside down world for me.

00:30:11.570 --> 00:30:16.320
I realized, oh, wow, all these things that
I have assumed are just the way things are

00:30:16.320 --> 00:30:22.289
done, I don't want to say arbitrary, but they're
social conventions.

00:30:22.289 --> 00:30:26.730
These are cultural emergent patterns.

00:30:26.730 --> 00:30:29.970
They're not laws of physics.

00:30:29.970 --> 00:30:37.120
During that same year, I also really, I think,
unlocked my ability to start to ask at least

00:30:37.120 --> 00:30:38.360
questions about my assumptions.

00:30:38.360 --> 00:30:39.360
Right?

00:30:39.360 --> 00:30:40.360
"Well, is that really true?

00:30:40.360 --> 00:30:41.429
Do we really have to do that?"

00:30:41.429 --> 00:30:42.429
Jonathan Haidt: Yeah.

00:30:42.429 --> 00:30:43.429
Tim Ferriss: "Is this really how it works?"

00:30:43.429 --> 00:30:45.559
Even the structure of thought is mirrored
in language.

00:30:45.559 --> 00:30:47.039
I mean, Japanese is very, very different.

00:30:47.039 --> 00:30:52.241
Instead of being subject verb object, "I eat
the apple," "I the apple eat," "I to school

00:30:52.241 --> 00:30:54.779
go," that type of thing.

00:30:54.779 --> 00:30:57.210
It was a very productive year.

00:30:57.210 --> 00:30:59.480
I want to come to a passing —
Jonathan Haidt: Wait, hold on a second.

00:30:59.480 --> 00:31:00.799
I just want to pick up on that for one moment.

00:31:00.799 --> 00:31:04.059
I think you had your experience at a sort
of a more opportune age than mine.

00:31:04.059 --> 00:31:07.320
I mean, mine worked out great, but you were
in college at the time.

00:31:07.320 --> 00:31:08.330
You were a student.

00:31:08.330 --> 00:31:10.800
Tim Ferriss: Actually, this was before Princeton.

00:31:10.800 --> 00:31:13.590
This would've been high school.

00:31:13.590 --> 00:31:15.810
This was age 15 to 16.

00:31:15.810 --> 00:31:16.810
Jonathan Haidt: Okay.

00:31:16.810 --> 00:31:17.810
Yeah.

00:31:17.810 --> 00:31:18.810
All right.

00:31:18.810 --> 00:31:20.220
There's research on how we create stories
about ourselves.

00:31:20.220 --> 00:31:24.870
And our stories are wide open from about 14
or 15 is when it kind of opens up through

00:31:24.870 --> 00:31:26.100
the mid to late twenties.

00:31:26.100 --> 00:31:28.230
That's like the key period.

00:31:28.230 --> 00:31:32.460
You had yours right at the beginning of that
and listening to you, because I think so much

00:31:32.460 --> 00:31:36.670
about Left-Right differences, there's research
that foreign travel makes people more Progressive,

00:31:36.670 --> 00:31:40.419
more Liberal because you see how things can
be different.

00:31:40.419 --> 00:31:41.700
Did it have that effect on you?

00:31:41.700 --> 00:31:43.679
Were you Progressive or Liberal at the time?

00:31:43.679 --> 00:31:45.600
Did it change your politics at all?

00:31:45.600 --> 00:31:47.269
Tim Ferriss: I am grateful.

00:31:47.269 --> 00:31:48.269
Very, very grateful.

00:31:48.269 --> 00:31:56.220
I don't want to throw either of my parents
under the bus, but my dad was very vocal about

00:31:56.220 --> 00:32:02.529
various political stuff and I completely turned
off that listening function.

00:32:02.529 --> 00:32:10.279
So I did not, this is going to be embarrassing
perhaps to admit, but I effectively paid little

00:32:10.279 --> 00:32:14.299
to no attention to politics until perhaps
10 years ago.

00:32:14.299 --> 00:32:18.370
So I didn't identify politically at all.

00:32:18.370 --> 00:32:22.610
I was really issue by issue, if they came
up.

00:32:22.610 --> 00:32:26.350
If it was something that affected me or that
I thought I might want to change, that was

00:32:26.350 --> 00:32:29.710
the extent of my radar pickup.

00:32:29.710 --> 00:32:39.860
I will say that in retrospect, looking at
my experience, probably also given the way

00:32:39.860 --> 00:32:44.460
that Japanese society is structured and the
availability of healthcare and this, that,

00:32:44.460 --> 00:32:52.500
and the other thing, that many of those issues
would probably be associated with a Left-leaning,

00:32:52.500 --> 00:32:54.980
Progressive mindset, I suppose.

00:32:54.980 --> 00:32:56.740
I would have to imagine that to be the case.

00:32:56.740 --> 00:32:59.690
I don't see how it would go the other way
because I wasn't — even though in Japan

00:32:59.690 --> 00:33:08.210
there is some pretty crazy nationalism and
super hardcore, I suppose, Right-extreme stuff,

00:33:08.210 --> 00:33:09.750
I was not exposed to that.

00:33:09.750 --> 00:33:10.750
Jonathan Haidt: Right.

00:33:10.750 --> 00:33:11.750
Okay.

00:33:11.750 --> 00:33:13.690
Because the thought, the idea that I was just
developing from what you were saying was this,

00:33:13.690 --> 00:33:17.299
I've long thought, since I wrote The Righteous
Mind, and since I really tried to understand

00:33:17.299 --> 00:33:21.890
Conservatism and Libertarians and really got
the wisdom from those traditions, as well

00:33:21.890 --> 00:33:26.750
as the Progressive or Liberal tradition, and
I've really come to see that a functioning

00:33:26.750 --> 00:33:31.419
society, it needs a Progressive wing pushing
for change and it needs a Conservative wing

00:33:31.419 --> 00:33:34.370
saying, "Slow down," tapping on the brakes.

00:33:34.370 --> 00:33:37.030
William F. Buckley stands athwart history,
yelling, "Stop."

00:33:37.030 --> 00:33:39.039
You need both in a healthy society.

00:33:39.039 --> 00:33:40.120
We don't have that now.

00:33:40.120 --> 00:33:41.370
Our Left is not Liberal.

00:33:41.370 --> 00:33:42.770
Our Right is not Conservative.

00:33:42.770 --> 00:33:43.770
We're a mess.

00:33:43.770 --> 00:33:45.750
But societies need those two impulses.

00:33:45.750 --> 00:33:48.990
And just from when you were saying, it didn't
occur to me, it's like, actually, I wonder

00:33:48.990 --> 00:33:51.540
if in a person's development you need both
of those.

00:33:51.540 --> 00:33:58.090
That is, early on you need to open up and
travel and see the world and question things.

00:33:58.090 --> 00:34:02.570
But if you stay that way the rest of your
life, you might be dysfunctional.

00:34:02.570 --> 00:34:06.060
Because there's interesting research showing
that it's not age that makes people become

00:34:06.060 --> 00:34:07.440
more Conservative.

00:34:07.440 --> 00:34:11.179
It's having children or starting a business
or taking on responsibility.

00:34:11.179 --> 00:34:13.639
Once you take on some responsibility, you
realize, "You know what?

00:34:13.639 --> 00:34:15.200
We actually need some rules here.

00:34:15.200 --> 00:34:18.169
There has to be some authority or some hierarchy.

00:34:18.169 --> 00:34:20.679
There have to be punishments for cheating."

00:34:20.679 --> 00:34:24.780
So in the course of a human life, maybe there's
the opening up travel period and then the

00:34:24.780 --> 00:34:27.669
like, okay, you've got all this stuff in your
head, now you have to apply it.

00:34:27.669 --> 00:34:29.450
You have to focus and you have to be conscientious.

00:34:29.450 --> 00:34:30.579
All right, just a thought.

00:34:30.579 --> 00:34:31.579
Keep going.

00:34:31.579 --> 00:34:32.579
Tim Ferriss: I like that idea.

00:34:32.579 --> 00:34:34.860
I've never thought of it that way.

00:34:34.860 --> 00:34:45.169
I would also say Japan deeply resonated with
me on a lot of levels that my experience in

00:34:45.169 --> 00:34:47.800
the United States did not.

00:34:47.800 --> 00:34:58.830
The cleanliness, the obsessive compulsive
focus on detail, just the level of meticulous

00:34:58.830 --> 00:35:04.890
attention to the most minute of details, which
can be crippling.

00:35:04.890 --> 00:35:14.420
It's not all upside, but that so deeply, to
me, felt like a warm bath for maybe my predispositions

00:35:14.420 --> 00:35:18.750
or my neurosis, that it felt like a rebirth
of sorts.

00:35:18.750 --> 00:35:21.530
I would have friends who would say, "[foreign
language]."

00:35:21.530 --> 00:35:25.290
They would just be like, "You're not American.

00:35:25.290 --> 00:35:27.240
I don't get it.

00:35:27.240 --> 00:35:33.220
You're white and you look like what you look
like, but you're more Japanese than I am."

00:35:33.220 --> 00:35:40.730
They would make these jokes because it came
so easily to me culturally in a way to whatever

00:35:40.730 --> 00:35:44.000
extent that makes any sense, there was that.

00:35:44.000 --> 00:35:53.270
I did want to ask you about your comment,
the label you used of Jewish atheist.

00:35:53.270 --> 00:35:59.890
So "I am a Jewish atheist," and I want to
provide some context for this, which is Paul

00:35:59.890 --> 00:36:07.500
Graham, who's famous for co-founding Y Combinator,
had a tweet, I think it was actually in one

00:36:07.500 --> 00:36:13.380
of his essays also, along the lines of, "The
more labels you apply to yourself, the stupider

00:36:13.380 --> 00:36:14.380
you become."

00:36:14.380 --> 00:36:15.380
Something like that.

00:36:15.380 --> 00:36:17.589
I'm paraphrasing it, but, be careful what
the labels that you use.

00:36:17.589 --> 00:36:24.030
I'm not forcing that on you, but I'm curious
to know the thinking behind Jewish atheist.

00:36:24.030 --> 00:36:26.599
I'm friends with a writer named A.J.

00:36:26.599 --> 00:36:28.609
Jacobs, who said —
Jonathan Haidt: Oh, yeah.

00:36:28.609 --> 00:36:29.609
I love A.J.

00:36:29.609 --> 00:36:30.609
Yeah.

00:36:30.609 --> 00:36:31.609
One of the funniest writers around.

00:36:31.609 --> 00:36:33.270
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, he's hilarious.

00:36:33.270 --> 00:36:42.240
So he said something like, "I am to Jewish
what Olive Garden is to Italian," or something

00:36:42.240 --> 00:36:44.770
like that, was his explanation —
Jonathan Haidt: Yeah, that's a [inaudible],

00:36:44.770 --> 00:36:45.770
yeah.

00:36:45.770 --> 00:36:49.060
Tim Ferriss: — when he wrote the book The
Year of Living Biblically, which is a fantastic,

00:36:49.060 --> 00:36:50.510
fantastic book.

00:36:50.510 --> 00:36:53.630
How do you think about being Jewish?

00:36:53.630 --> 00:36:55.990
What does that mean to you?

00:36:55.990 --> 00:37:01.200
Jonathan Haidt: If we're having this discussion
in the context of a Christian background culture,

00:37:01.200 --> 00:37:06.190
Christianity is somewhat unusual, although
Islam is similar in making — it's all about

00:37:06.190 --> 00:37:08.880
your relationship with God and faith.

00:37:08.880 --> 00:37:16.480
And Judaism is a more ancient religion, and
it's very much about a story about a tribe,

00:37:16.480 --> 00:37:20.089
a group, that has these ups and downs in history
and we're bound together.

00:37:20.089 --> 00:37:25.170
Of course, God is a very, very important part
of the story, but as Jews have lived in different

00:37:25.170 --> 00:37:30.140
cultures around the world, well that shapes
your identity and so you can be Jewish and

00:37:30.140 --> 00:37:34.130
not believe in God, just like you can be Italian
and not be Catholic.

00:37:34.130 --> 00:37:40.730
So as an ethnic group, as an identity, as
a culture, I'm Jewish, and I recognize the

00:37:40.730 --> 00:37:43.680
influence of just the norms.

00:37:43.680 --> 00:37:44.680
The argumentativeness.

00:37:44.680 --> 00:37:47.619
And here we're talking Ashkenazi Jewish.

00:37:47.619 --> 00:37:51.980
I should always be clear that Sephardic Jews
are different.

00:37:51.980 --> 00:37:55.109
There are different kinds of Jews, but the
American Jewish experience is dominated by

00:37:55.109 --> 00:37:57.390
the Ashkenazi, the Eastern European immigrants.

00:37:57.390 --> 00:38:02.020
All four of my grandparents came here from
Russia, Poland, and Belarus around 1907 when

00:38:02.020 --> 00:38:04.210
they were teenagers, roughly.

00:38:04.210 --> 00:38:10.130
So, yeah, it's part of my — okay, so I was
about to say, it's part of my identity, and

00:38:10.130 --> 00:38:11.130
I guess it is.

00:38:11.130 --> 00:38:14.050
But I'm playing these days with the idea that
actually I'm an anti-identitarian.

00:38:14.050 --> 00:38:19.420
I think part of what's wrong with the country
is that America, of all countries, needs to

00:38:19.420 --> 00:38:20.420
be a melting pot.

00:38:20.420 --> 00:38:24.210
We need to have a generally assimilationist
ethos.

00:38:24.210 --> 00:38:29.050
And that worked so well for the Jews, the
Irish, the Italians, all the groups that came

00:38:29.050 --> 00:38:33.330
through Ellis Island into an assimilationist,
20th century America.

00:38:33.330 --> 00:38:35.690
It worked out fantastically well.

00:38:35.690 --> 00:38:40.090
Anyway, we can follow that down in a moment,
the identitarianism, but I guess, so you can

00:38:40.090 --> 00:38:42.300
be Jewish by identity and not believe in God.

00:38:42.300 --> 00:38:43.819
In fact, I'd like to do this formally.

00:38:43.819 --> 00:38:47.030
I always think if you ask a Christian, "Do
you believe in God?"

00:38:47.030 --> 00:38:51.950
Usually, if they've identified as a Christian,
they're usually going to say, "Yes."

00:38:51.950 --> 00:38:55.210
Okay, try asking that with 10 Jews.

00:38:55.210 --> 00:38:58.790
Maybe one of them will say, "Yes," and maybe
two or three will say, "No."

00:38:58.790 --> 00:39:00.319
And the rest will say, "Well, it depends.

00:39:00.319 --> 00:39:02.030
Do you mean this?

00:39:02.030 --> 00:39:03.030
Do you that?

00:39:03.030 --> 00:39:04.030
Well, sort of, maybe."

00:39:04.030 --> 00:39:07.870
So being Jewish is different religiously than
being Christian or Muslim.

00:39:07.870 --> 00:39:08.870
Tim Ferriss: Mm-hmm.

00:39:08.870 --> 00:39:09.870
That makes sense.

00:39:09.870 --> 00:39:10.900
That makes sense to me.

00:39:10.900 --> 00:39:23.011
Do you, as an atheist, build in any rituals
or traditions from the Jewish lineage, let's

00:39:23.011 --> 00:39:24.011
say?

00:39:24.011 --> 00:39:27.760
Jonathan Haidt: Okay, so this helps me clarify,
atheist is not part of my identity.

00:39:27.760 --> 00:39:34.020
I said I'm a Jewish atheist, but atheist is
not part of my identity.

00:39:34.020 --> 00:39:36.900
I'm just a Jew who doesn't believe in God.

00:39:36.900 --> 00:39:41.380
And that maybe was part of my clash with Sam
Harris, because this was during the time of

00:39:41.380 --> 00:39:45.750
the new atheists and there was a combative
movement, and I didn't want to be part of

00:39:45.750 --> 00:39:46.750
that.

00:39:46.750 --> 00:39:48.760
I thought there was a lot to respect about
religion.

00:39:48.760 --> 00:39:52.830
So I would do the Shweder thing, which is,
"Okay, you're making these claims about religion.

00:39:52.830 --> 00:39:53.830
Well, let's see.

00:39:53.830 --> 00:39:57.030
Turn that around, is that really true?"

00:39:57.030 --> 00:40:04.270
But back to your question, so no, I don't
incorporate any rituals that are related to

00:40:04.270 --> 00:40:07.880
my being an atheist, but I do, yeah, I do
have some rituals.

00:40:07.880 --> 00:40:10.580
But this actually almost more comes from Stoic
traditions.

00:40:10.580 --> 00:40:17.650
Just that the Stoics advise doing a morning
routine, an evening routine.

00:40:17.650 --> 00:40:21.290
Meditations on philosophy in the morning,
and then a taking stock at night.

00:40:21.290 --> 00:40:27.780
So I usually try to do that just as an individual
practice to just set my priorities and reflect

00:40:27.780 --> 00:40:31.450
on my day and what I'm doing.

00:40:31.450 --> 00:40:36.839
And minimally Jewish in the sense of, we go,
my family — my wife is Korean-American,

00:40:36.839 --> 00:40:39.990
but she was very happy to have us raise our
kids with some Jewish identity.

00:40:39.990 --> 00:40:43.290
My son had a bar mitzvah.

00:40:43.290 --> 00:40:45.579
We go to synagogue on the High Holy Days.

00:40:45.579 --> 00:40:48.579
We light Shabbat candles and have a family
meal on Friday.

00:40:48.579 --> 00:40:49.740
So fairly minimal.

00:40:49.740 --> 00:40:54.680
But it is important, I do have a feeling that
something was passed down to the generations

00:40:54.680 --> 00:40:57.370
to me, and I don't want to break the chain.

00:40:57.370 --> 00:41:00.310
It's a very valuable thing that was passed
down to me and I want my children to have

00:41:00.310 --> 00:41:02.480
a sense that they're part of that chain.

00:41:02.480 --> 00:41:08.390
Tim Ferriss: Let's talk about from feud to
friends with Sam Harris.

00:41:08.390 --> 00:41:13.579
What was, and I know you described it in passing,
but what was the feud?

00:41:13.579 --> 00:41:18.790
And then the more interesting part of that
is, I shouldn't say interesting, but the part

00:41:18.790 --> 00:41:23.510
that I want to focus on is, how did you go
from that to then friends?

00:41:23.510 --> 00:41:30.030
Because that is — feuds are where things
start and end a lot of the time these days,

00:41:30.030 --> 00:41:35.910
and maybe forever given human nature, but
certainly online people get rewarded probably

00:41:35.910 --> 00:41:39.670
more for feuding than they do for being friendly.

00:41:39.670 --> 00:41:44.180
So could you just describe —
Jonathan Haidt: Oh, sure.

00:41:44.180 --> 00:41:46.329
Tim Ferriss: — that experience, please?

00:41:46.329 --> 00:41:52.470
Jonathan Haidt: Back when Sam really burst
onto the scene, he's a great writer and a

00:41:52.470 --> 00:41:56.730
beautiful writer, and he was so persuasive
writing those first two atheist, new atheist

00:41:56.730 --> 00:41:57.730
books.

00:41:57.730 --> 00:42:00.599
We would be invited to a lot of the same conferences
because I was beginning to write about how

00:42:00.599 --> 00:42:06.220
religion — I'm a naturalist, I believe we
evolved to be reli — I don't believe it's

00:42:06.220 --> 00:42:10.849
God who made us religious, but religion does
all these good things, I was saying.

00:42:10.849 --> 00:42:12.619
So we'd be invited to conferences together.

00:42:12.619 --> 00:42:15.050
There was the Beyond Belief Conference.

00:42:15.050 --> 00:42:19.390
Sam and all the other new atheists went the
first year, and then Sam and me and one or

00:42:19.390 --> 00:42:20.650
two others went the second year.

00:42:20.650 --> 00:42:25.460
And wherever I would appear with Sam, or wherever
we would clash in writing, Sam would always

00:42:25.460 --> 00:42:29.940
use this rhetorical technique of basically
saying, "So Jon Haidt would say that human

00:42:29.940 --> 00:42:32.849
sacrifice among the Aztecs is a good thing
because it binds people..."

00:42:32.849 --> 00:42:36.470
So he would always accuse me of supporting
atrocities.

00:42:36.470 --> 00:42:39.180
By the third or fourth time he'd done this,
I was, "Okay."

00:42:39.180 --> 00:42:40.670
I said before I don't get angry.

00:42:40.670 --> 00:42:42.800
I was kind of pissed.

00:42:42.800 --> 00:42:45.460
So I did a dirty lowdown trick.

00:42:45.460 --> 00:42:46.460
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, yeah.

00:42:46.460 --> 00:42:47.460
There must be some decorum.

00:42:47.460 --> 00:42:48.470
There must be some decorum.

00:42:48.470 --> 00:42:49.470
Yes.

00:42:49.470 --> 00:42:51.941
Jonathan Haidt: So I did something, I'm a
little embarrassed by it.

00:42:51.941 --> 00:42:54.270
It was clever, but I'm a little embarrassed
now.

00:42:54.270 --> 00:42:59.359
I did an academic trick, which is — one
thing that bothered me about Sam.

00:42:59.359 --> 00:43:03.960
Sam, he has a PhD, but he wasn't really socialized
as an academic.

00:43:03.960 --> 00:43:10.559
He was writing more as a rhetorician, a public
arguer.

00:43:10.559 --> 00:43:15.662
He would use very strong language, "always"
this and "never" this and "certainly" this.

00:43:15.662 --> 00:43:18.849
So I said, you know what?

00:43:18.849 --> 00:43:23.540
Let me take all of Sam's writings and feed
them through this, what's it called?

00:43:23.540 --> 00:43:24.540
Oh, shoot.

00:43:24.540 --> 00:43:26.880
There's a program that analyzes word use by
Jamie Pennebaker.

00:43:26.880 --> 00:43:30.490
Anyway, it's, you feed it through and it counts
"certainly," "always," "never."

00:43:30.490 --> 00:43:33.740
So there's a category called certainty.

00:43:33.740 --> 00:43:39.330
I fed a text of Sam's books through, and you
get a number, how certain is Sam?

00:43:39.330 --> 00:43:41.070
And a couple things like that.

00:43:41.070 --> 00:43:46.730
Then I fed in the other new atheists, and
then I fed in all of those, who are all those

00:43:46.730 --> 00:43:47.730
Right-wing people?

00:43:47.730 --> 00:43:50.780
Rush Limbaugh and Glenn Beck, and I put a
whole bunch of them in.

00:43:50.780 --> 00:43:56.940
Then I put in me and David Sloan Wilson, who
is also with me arguing that religion is nuanced

00:43:56.940 --> 00:43:57.940
effects.

00:43:57.940 --> 00:44:03.720
It turned out that Sam was higher on certainty
words than Glenn Beck and Rush Limbaugh and

00:44:03.720 --> 00:44:04.829
all those people.

00:44:04.829 --> 00:44:09.650
So I just wrote this up in a blog post, and
I showed graphically that Sam is actually

00:44:09.650 --> 00:44:14.140
more certain of himself than these people
that he's always lambasting.

00:44:14.140 --> 00:44:15.730
So it was a dirty trick, but it was funny.

00:44:15.730 --> 00:44:21.270
Tim Ferriss: And the closing of the first
round ends with a one-two combination.

00:44:21.270 --> 00:44:22.340
Jonathan Haidt: Yeah.

00:44:22.340 --> 00:44:26.420
Oh, and then, I think I offered, I called
it The Moral Psychology Prize or The Sam Harris

00:44:26.420 --> 00:44:27.870
Prize or something that — oh, yeah.

00:44:27.870 --> 00:44:28.870
Oh, yes.

00:44:28.870 --> 00:44:29.870
No, no, okay.

00:44:29.870 --> 00:44:30.870
I forgot a crucial thing.

00:44:30.870 --> 00:44:35.470
Sam wrote his book, The Moral Landscape, saying
morality is a fact.

00:44:35.470 --> 00:44:40.460
And he offered money, I think it was money,
to whoever could persuade him otherwise.

00:44:40.460 --> 00:44:46.390
Then I wrote my essay, I bet that no one will
be able to persuade Sam otherwise, and here's

00:44:46.390 --> 00:44:47.390
why.

00:44:47.390 --> 00:44:49.230
Then I showed that how certain he is.

00:44:49.230 --> 00:44:52.240
So I just put him in a bind that if he — anyway,
I can't remember exactly all the details.

00:44:52.240 --> 00:44:53.240
Anyway, so that's how it started.

00:44:53.240 --> 00:44:57.100
So then we had a little cold — we just ignored
each other for a while.

00:44:57.100 --> 00:45:03.900
And my recollection of how it ended was, Sam
is incredibly courageous and is not a partisan

00:45:03.900 --> 00:45:07.270
and he thinks for himself, and he does things
that make people hate him on both sides.

00:45:07.270 --> 00:45:09.020
I always respected him for that.

00:45:09.020 --> 00:45:13.200
It was something, I think it was, he said
something about Islam and he was being lambasted,

00:45:13.200 --> 00:45:17.810
and I can't remember whether I retweeted something
or said something.

00:45:17.810 --> 00:45:20.890
I put up some little tiny show of support
for him.

00:45:20.890 --> 00:45:22.480
I think that's how it went.

00:45:22.480 --> 00:45:25.589
And then Sam acknowledged it and thanked me
for it.

00:45:25.589 --> 00:45:28.839
What happened was, because our feud was in
2012, 2014, whatever it was.

00:45:28.839 --> 00:45:31.030
And that was back before everything went insane.

00:45:31.030 --> 00:45:34.490
And around 2014, 2015, everything went insane.

00:45:34.490 --> 00:45:41.160
There was this incredible explosion of illiberalism
on campus and in the academic world and the

00:45:41.160 --> 00:45:42.320
intellectual world.

00:45:42.320 --> 00:45:46.599
Sam and I were then actually both on the same
side, which is we believe in being reasonable

00:45:46.599 --> 00:45:48.220
and using evidence.

00:45:48.220 --> 00:45:53.430
So we ultimately were natural allies and whatever,
whoever first gave a little show of support

00:45:53.430 --> 00:45:55.940
for the other, it was like, "Okay, yeah, you
know what?

00:45:55.940 --> 00:45:56.940
I like you."

00:45:56.940 --> 00:46:01.180
I've been on his podcast twice and I have
just great admiration for him.

00:46:01.180 --> 00:46:03.750
Tim Ferriss: He's a very, very smart cookie.

00:46:03.750 --> 00:46:09.050
I'm also a very big fan of Sam.

00:46:09.050 --> 00:46:15.650
And flash boil, 2014, 2015, what the hell
happened?

00:46:15.650 --> 00:46:20.830
What contributed to things suddenly hockey
sticking in insanity?

00:46:20.830 --> 00:46:21.830
Could it have been predicted?

00:46:21.830 --> 00:46:22.830
Jonathan Haidt: No.

00:46:22.830 --> 00:46:24.100
No, I don't think so.

00:46:24.100 --> 00:46:28.309
I find these big ideas that really help me
think, and so I'm going to put another one

00:46:28.309 --> 00:46:31.140
out there, which is a complex dynamical system.

00:46:31.140 --> 00:46:33.180
Is this something you've talked about on your
show before?

00:46:33.180 --> 00:46:34.180
Tim Ferriss: No.

00:46:34.180 --> 00:46:35.180
Mm-mm.

00:46:35.180 --> 00:46:36.300
Jonathan Haidt: So complex dynamical system.

00:46:36.300 --> 00:46:41.780
The human mind is really good at thinking
about mechanical systems, machines, things

00:46:41.780 --> 00:46:43.740
moving in space.

00:46:43.740 --> 00:46:44.740
We have sort of two brains.

00:46:44.740 --> 00:46:45.740
We have sort of two minds.

00:46:45.740 --> 00:46:49.040
We have a mind that's really good at thinking
about objects moving in space.

00:46:49.040 --> 00:46:51.980
So there's Popular Mechanics for people who
like doing that.

00:46:51.980 --> 00:46:56.000
We're really good at thinking about people
moving in social space and forming alliances

00:46:56.000 --> 00:46:58.232
and betrayals and soap operas and all that.

00:46:58.232 --> 00:47:01.400
So there's People Magazine for people who
like doing that.

00:47:01.400 --> 00:47:04.560
Those two things we're really good at, but
there are a lot of things in our world that

00:47:04.560 --> 00:47:06.020
are nothing like that.

00:47:06.020 --> 00:47:07.740
Like the weather, the economy.

00:47:07.740 --> 00:47:12.250
There are complex dynamical systems where
you can't, even in principle, predict what

00:47:12.250 --> 00:47:15.680
it's going to do because it's a chaotic complex
system.

00:47:15.680 --> 00:47:19.900
You can change parameters and you can kind
of predict which way things are going to go,

00:47:19.900 --> 00:47:22.250
but you can't really know.

00:47:22.250 --> 00:47:28.130
When you change parameters, you can suddenly
get a phase change.

00:47:28.130 --> 00:47:29.780
Mechanical systems are easy to understand.

00:47:29.780 --> 00:47:34.750
Complex dynamical systems are very hard, but
we live within them.

00:47:34.750 --> 00:47:39.030
So what happened, I believe, is that we hit
a phase change.

00:47:39.030 --> 00:47:43.540
It actually began in 2014, but it blew up
in the academic world in 2015.

00:47:43.540 --> 00:47:48.670
This is what I've called metaphorically the
fall of the Tower of Babel.

00:47:48.670 --> 00:47:52.720
So if you simply wire people up, if you just
connect people more and more, you think that's

00:47:52.720 --> 00:47:54.140
good.

00:47:54.140 --> 00:47:55.859
We always thought that.

00:47:55.859 --> 00:48:01.130
From the Roman roads and the first telegram
cables or the Pony Express, it's good to have

00:48:01.130 --> 00:48:03.670
more communication, that's generally a good
thing.

00:48:03.670 --> 00:48:05.579
But it could have some problems.

00:48:05.579 --> 00:48:10.380
It might be that you just get to a point where
everyone's so connected that everyone is yelling

00:48:10.380 --> 00:48:12.170
or everyone's mad at everyone.

00:48:12.170 --> 00:48:14.900
So it might just be that there's more and
more connection, but I think it's something

00:48:14.900 --> 00:48:15.900
else.

00:48:15.900 --> 00:48:21.230
What I've come to believe in trying to understand
what the hell happened is it's the nature

00:48:21.230 --> 00:48:25.450
of the connection changed after 2009.

00:48:25.450 --> 00:48:32.089
Once, we used to call social network sites,
they connected people, so you could use Facebook

00:48:32.089 --> 00:48:33.750
to communicate with your friends from high
school.

00:48:33.750 --> 00:48:34.750
That was great.

00:48:34.750 --> 00:48:35.770
There's no problem there.

00:48:35.770 --> 00:48:39.050
But in 2009, Facebook introduced the like
button.

00:48:39.050 --> 00:48:42.300
Twitter introduced the retweet button, Facebook
copied it with the share button.

00:48:42.300 --> 00:48:45.060
So social media isn't just connecting people
anymore.

00:48:45.060 --> 00:48:48.810
We stopped talking about them as social networking
sites.

00:48:48.810 --> 00:48:52.109
We start using the word social media platform.

00:48:52.109 --> 00:48:57.869
Now it becomes about performance and it becomes
much more viral, much more explosive.

00:48:57.869 --> 00:49:03.790
It's not about me talking with you, it's about
me talking at you in the hopes that my slam

00:49:03.790 --> 00:49:06.690
on you will get picked up by others, and that
will make me famous.

00:49:06.690 --> 00:49:09.160
That will get me prestige.

00:49:09.160 --> 00:49:12.490
Once everyone's motivated to do that, or I
should say once 10 percent of people are motivated

00:49:12.490 --> 00:49:18.480
to do that, the system develops new properties
that were not there in 2008.

00:49:18.480 --> 00:49:22.550
Most of us, most of your listeners, if they're
old enough, can remember it used to be an

00:49:22.550 --> 00:49:23.550
amazing place.

00:49:23.550 --> 00:49:24.990
The internet was amazing.

00:49:24.990 --> 00:49:27.070
Social media was amazing.

00:49:27.070 --> 00:49:29.460
Twitter was playful and fun.

00:49:29.460 --> 00:49:32.799
It's still that in part, but it just became
much nastier.

00:49:32.799 --> 00:49:37.290
One of the key innovations, I believe, was
what Facebook called threaded comments.

00:49:37.290 --> 00:49:43.250
So it used to be if Barack Obama puts up a
Tweet in his first term, he puts it up and

00:49:43.250 --> 00:49:45.520
people can respond and that's it.

00:49:45.520 --> 00:49:46.600
They just respond and that's it.

00:49:46.600 --> 00:49:49.690
You can say, "You're stupid," or, "You're
terrible," and that's it.

00:49:49.690 --> 00:49:51.160
You say that.

00:49:51.160 --> 00:49:56.390
But in 2014, Facebook said, "How about, to
increase engagement, how about we let everyone

00:49:56.390 --> 00:49:58.590
fight with everyone everywhere?"

00:49:58.590 --> 00:50:04.890
So if Barack Obama puts up a Facebook post
in his second term, or after 2014, someone

00:50:04.890 --> 00:50:07.349
can say, "Go back to Kenya."

00:50:07.349 --> 00:50:11.559
And then someone else can say to that commenter,
"No, you are a fascist."

00:50:11.559 --> 00:50:14.290
And then before you know it, you've got people
fighting in the comments.

00:50:14.290 --> 00:50:18.360
So it's like those scenes in a barroom brawl,
you've got two people having a face off and

00:50:18.360 --> 00:50:20.450
suddenly everyone's breaking bottles in everyone's
heads.

00:50:20.450 --> 00:50:23.680
That, I believe, is what happened in 2014,
2015.

00:50:23.680 --> 00:50:28.730
Democracy is unsustainable, institutions are
collapsing, universities are going crazy.

00:50:28.730 --> 00:50:32.330
So we're now in the post Babel world where
we can't have any shared understandings of

00:50:32.330 --> 00:50:33.330
truth.

00:50:33.330 --> 00:50:34.510
We can't have any shared stories.

00:50:34.510 --> 00:50:40.799
Hence the title of my forthcoming book, Life
after Babel: Adapting to a World We Can No

00:50:40.799 --> 00:50:41.799
Longer Share.

00:50:41.799 --> 00:50:51.480
Tim Ferriss: Do you have thoughts on what
we can do from a proactive incentive perspective

00:50:51.480 --> 00:50:58.369
to, I don't want to say reverse some of these
trends, but to redirect the currents slightly

00:50:58.369 --> 00:50:59.660
in different directions?

00:50:59.660 --> 00:51:01.299
And you mentioned Chicago.

00:51:01.299 --> 00:51:04.960
One thing that really jumped out to me, this
is quite a few years ago now I want to say,

00:51:04.960 --> 00:51:16.030
but when the woke supremacy virus started
spreading throughout academia and fragility

00:51:16.030 --> 00:51:21.580
was being reinforced and tripled down upon,
I want to say it was the University of Chicago,

00:51:21.580 --> 00:51:23.230
put out a letter to say —
Jonathan Haidt: Oh, yeah.

00:51:23.230 --> 00:51:24.230
Mm-hmm.

00:51:24.230 --> 00:51:26.790
Tim Ferriss: — "We are actually going to
go in the opposite direction."

00:51:26.790 --> 00:51:32.160
It was notable to me because a number of friends
of mine who are alumni from U of Chicago said,

00:51:32.160 --> 00:51:36.400
"This is the first time in 10 years that I've
given money to the university."

00:51:36.400 --> 00:51:40.260
So there are, I guess, a number of questions
in this word salad that I'm throwing at you.

00:51:40.260 --> 00:51:46.270
The first is, why do you think U of Chicago
was able to do that then, or willing to do

00:51:46.270 --> 00:51:47.270
it?

00:51:47.270 --> 00:51:50.760
And would they be willing to do it again now?

00:51:50.760 --> 00:51:55.030
Just to understand the incentives, because
I've seen so many people lose their posts,

00:51:55.030 --> 00:51:59.680
doesn't seem to matter how high up you are,
if you can be removed by the trustees, you

00:51:59.680 --> 00:52:07.440
are vulnerable, or whatever mechanism exists
for replacing the top brass.

00:52:07.440 --> 00:52:14.829
Then separately, let's focus on the academic
side first, and then we can look at the technological

00:52:14.829 --> 00:52:17.710
side and maybe the business side.

00:52:17.710 --> 00:52:23.910
So in the academic world, why do you think
it was possible for U of Chicago to do it

00:52:23.910 --> 00:52:24.910
then?

00:52:24.910 --> 00:52:25.910
Could they do it now?

00:52:25.910 --> 00:52:32.599
And is it possible to create some type of
counterbalance or change incentives such that

00:52:32.599 --> 00:52:34.930
this doesn't continue to go in the direction
it's going?

00:52:34.930 --> 00:52:35.990
Jonathan Haidt: Yes.

00:52:35.990 --> 00:52:42.430
I can't answer that question until I explain
the dynamics that have made leaders cave throughout

00:52:42.430 --> 00:52:47.930
the intellectual world, throughout the, let's
call it, we have democratic institutions and

00:52:47.930 --> 00:52:49.720
we have epistemic institutions.

00:52:49.720 --> 00:52:51.010
Epistemic meaning in search of knowledge.

00:52:51.010 --> 00:52:56.900
So that's preeminently the universities in
journalism, museums, other things like that.

00:52:56.900 --> 00:52:59.059
Why have they all fallen in the same way?

00:52:59.059 --> 00:53:03.849
Let me start with that and then we'll get
to Chicago.

00:53:03.849 --> 00:53:06.079
I've been trying to figure this out every
day.

00:53:06.079 --> 00:53:09.530
I've been obsessed with this since it started
in 2014.

00:53:09.530 --> 00:53:11.240
That's when Greg Lukianoff came to me.

00:53:11.240 --> 00:53:14.150
Greg is the President of The Foundation for
Individual Rights in Education.

00:53:14.150 --> 00:53:17.840
He said, "Jon, weird stuff is happening on
campus.

00:53:17.840 --> 00:53:21.660
Students are suddenly asking for protections
from books, word speakers, ideas."

00:53:21.660 --> 00:53:22.740
Before then it was always administrators.

00:53:22.740 --> 00:53:25.180
So Greg says, "What's going on?

00:53:25.180 --> 00:53:30.339
The students seem to be explaining these things
using the same cognitive distortions that

00:53:30.339 --> 00:53:33.770
I learned to not do when I was treated for
depression."

00:53:33.770 --> 00:53:36.660
Greg suffers from depression and he learned
cognitive behavioral therapy.

00:53:36.660 --> 00:53:39.930
Anyway, so I thought Greg's idea was brilliant.

00:53:39.930 --> 00:53:45.750
Somehow universities are teaching students
to think, to catastrophize, overgeneralize,

00:53:45.750 --> 00:53:47.069
mind reading, all these distortions.

00:53:47.069 --> 00:53:54.619
So that starts in 2014 and then it's really
the fall of 2015, especially after the Halloween

00:53:54.619 --> 00:54:01.290
incident at Yale where the students demand
that Nicholas Christakis apologize for a thoughtful,

00:54:01.290 --> 00:54:04.819
caring letter that his wife had written to
the students of Silliman College.

00:54:04.819 --> 00:54:09.299
Then the President of Yale, Peter Salovey
never backed up the Christakises.

00:54:09.299 --> 00:54:14.100
They demanded that they be fired, and the
president just gave the students everything

00:54:14.100 --> 00:54:15.119
they demanded that he could.

00:54:15.119 --> 00:54:19.569
He couldn't give them everything, but he really
made a big show of saying, "Yes, I will.

00:54:19.569 --> 00:54:20.569
You're right."

00:54:20.569 --> 00:54:22.980
And that launched a wave of protests around
the country.

00:54:22.980 --> 00:54:29.190
It always goes the same way, which is a charge
from a student that something that was said

00:54:29.190 --> 00:54:35.760
was insensitive about race, gender, LGBTQ,
and then there's never a finding of information.

00:54:35.760 --> 00:54:40.059
It blows up on Twitter or other channels,
and then within a day or two, the person's

00:54:40.059 --> 00:54:43.309
fired, no due process.

00:54:43.309 --> 00:54:44.309
This keeps going.

00:54:44.309 --> 00:54:48.380
It happened at The New York Times, it just
happened at the Association for Psychological

00:54:48.380 --> 00:54:49.380
Science here.

00:54:49.380 --> 00:54:51.540
An editor may have made a mistake.

00:54:51.540 --> 00:54:53.470
It looks like he probably didn't follow good
process.

00:54:53.470 --> 00:54:55.799
But again, there's a mob and then the leadership
gives in.

00:54:55.799 --> 00:54:57.119
So that's what's been happening.

00:54:57.119 --> 00:54:58.839
Tim Ferriss: Why are they all caving?

00:54:58.839 --> 00:55:03.630
I'm just trying to think of the incentives,
because it doesn't happen everywhere.

00:55:03.630 --> 00:55:04.630
Right?

00:55:04.630 --> 00:55:05.630
Jonathan Haidt: Right.

00:55:05.630 --> 00:55:06.630
Tim Ferriss: There are some counterexamples.

00:55:06.630 --> 00:55:07.630
Yeah.

00:55:07.630 --> 00:55:12.720
Jonathan Haidt: It happens when you get moral
homogeneity.

00:55:12.720 --> 00:55:17.710
So you get an institution where the great
majority, or almost everybody's on one side.

00:55:17.710 --> 00:55:20.170
In epistemic institutions, it's always going
to be the Left.

00:55:20.170 --> 00:55:26.390
Now you can have a craziness on an all Right-wing
institution, but there aren't any institutions

00:55:26.390 --> 00:55:30.750
on the Right that are as high, that have so
little diversity as those on the Left.

00:55:30.750 --> 00:55:34.280
On the Left, it's often the case that there's
not a single person on the Right.

00:55:34.280 --> 00:55:38.140
I rarely encounter a Conservative in the academic
world, they're very, very few.

00:55:38.140 --> 00:55:43.059
Whereas when I've spoken at military installations,
or if you have a Right-leaning thing, there's

00:55:43.059 --> 00:55:44.630
still going to be some Liberals there.

00:55:44.630 --> 00:55:48.599
Anyway, so you have moral homogeneity, which
is a crucial background variable.

00:55:48.599 --> 00:55:54.581
In moral homogeneity, you have students, so
usually it's members of Gen — oh, you have

00:55:54.581 --> 00:55:55.581
Gen Z.

00:55:55.581 --> 00:56:01.000
So the charges are usually from Gen Z or late
Millennials making a charge that the leadership

00:56:01.000 --> 00:56:07.080
is vulnerable to, because the leadership is
usually older white Liberals, and it's backed

00:56:07.080 --> 00:56:10.080
up with a barrage of darts.

00:56:10.080 --> 00:56:14.160
A dart is anything that someone can do to
hurt someone else.

00:56:14.160 --> 00:56:17.760
You can launch a dart on Twitter, you can
make an accusation, you have no liability

00:56:17.760 --> 00:56:18.760
for it.

00:56:18.760 --> 00:56:23.180
So what you see is these leaders, suddenly
they're in morally homogeneous, politically

00:56:23.180 --> 00:56:27.400
homogeneous institutions.

00:56:27.400 --> 00:56:29.610
They're attacked with darts, they cave instantly.

00:56:29.610 --> 00:56:32.000
It's very painful.

00:56:32.000 --> 00:56:36.750
They cave instantly, that just encourages
the darters to go further.

00:56:36.750 --> 00:56:40.849
So I called this in a recent Atlantic article,
I called this structural stupidity.

00:56:40.849 --> 00:56:45.910
That is, you can have very smart people, but
you only get smart when bad ideas will be

00:56:45.910 --> 00:56:51.839
challenged, and if a bad idea — so for example,
at universities, we all now have bias response

00:56:51.839 --> 00:56:52.839
teams.

00:56:52.839 --> 00:56:56.620
So if I say anything and a student objects,
they can report me in three ways.

00:56:56.620 --> 00:57:00.619
That number is posted in the bathrooms, it's
on the back of all the ID cards, like, "Here's

00:57:00.619 --> 00:57:01.619
911.

00:57:01.619 --> 00:57:02.619
Here's campus safety.

00:57:02.619 --> 00:57:03.619
Here's the bias response line.

00:57:03.619 --> 00:57:05.160
If you're offended, call this number."

00:57:05.160 --> 00:57:07.490
This is a really, really bad idea.

00:57:07.490 --> 00:57:10.180
Or at least, there's no evidence that it's
a good idea.

00:57:10.180 --> 00:57:11.560
But it's done everywhere.

00:57:11.560 --> 00:57:14.840
It's done everywhere, because no one dares
oppose it.

00:57:14.840 --> 00:57:17.870
Nobody in the faculty senate will say, "Well,
wait a second, do we really want to do this?"

00:57:17.870 --> 00:57:20.030
Everyone's just like, "Oh, yeah, let's do
this.

00:57:20.030 --> 00:57:22.180
This will be anti-racist."

00:57:22.180 --> 00:57:26.240
So you get structurally stupid communities
and they all fold in exactly the same way.

00:57:26.240 --> 00:57:28.750
This is what happened at The New York Times
with the firing of James Bennet.

00:57:28.750 --> 00:57:30.430
It's the same thing everywhere.

00:57:30.430 --> 00:57:32.160
Why was Chicago different?

00:57:32.160 --> 00:57:33.930
Two reasons.

00:57:33.930 --> 00:57:37.000
So every institution has its own moral resources.

00:57:37.000 --> 00:57:39.020
Every institution has a sense of identity.

00:57:39.020 --> 00:57:46.270
Chicago is unique in having this really intense
intellectual, really high intellectual identity.

00:57:46.270 --> 00:57:51.250
When I was there, students wore t-shirts,
"University of Chicago: where fun goes to

00:57:51.250 --> 00:57:52.250
die."

00:57:52.250 --> 00:57:56.760
They were ranked, Playboy Magazine ranked
them the least, the worst party school in

00:57:56.760 --> 00:58:00.560
America, 300th out of 300, don't go to Chicago.

00:58:00.560 --> 00:58:01.940
And they were proud of it.

00:58:01.940 --> 00:58:07.099
So they begin with this intellectually intense
identity, the background, and then the other

00:58:07.099 --> 00:58:08.980
crucial element was leadership.

00:58:08.980 --> 00:58:11.530
They had Robert Zimmer, that's it.

00:58:11.530 --> 00:58:14.869
Robert Zimmer personally was calling bullshit
on all this stuff.

00:58:14.869 --> 00:58:16.960
Like, "This is not who we are.

00:58:16.960 --> 00:58:21.280
This, the caving, the demands, the outrage
mobs, no, we're going to talk about ideas

00:58:21.280 --> 00:58:22.280
here."

00:58:22.280 --> 00:58:25.700
Robert Zimmer single-handedly said, "No."

00:58:25.700 --> 00:58:27.890
And that letter that you referred to was not
from him.

00:58:27.890 --> 00:58:32.550
It was by a dean of students who sent a letter
to prospective students who are coming to

00:58:32.550 --> 00:58:33.970
Chicago.

00:58:33.970 --> 00:58:36.339
He said, "At Chicago, we don't do safe space."

00:58:36.339 --> 00:58:41.220
Now he misspoke, that was a mistake, because
if the gay students or LGBT students want

00:58:41.220 --> 00:58:46.180
to have a club where they have certain discourse
rules, great, free association, let them.

00:58:46.180 --> 00:58:51.260
What the dean meant to say was, "Our classrooms
are not safe spaces.

00:58:51.260 --> 00:58:55.960
In our classrooms people can criticize what
you say."

00:58:55.960 --> 00:58:57.980
If you have that, then you don't have structural
stupidity.

00:58:57.980 --> 00:59:03.829
Tim Ferriss: So if we're looking at structural
stupidity, I start to think about structural

00:59:03.829 --> 00:59:12.350
dynamics and the ways in which people are
vulnerable or less vulnerable to losing their

00:59:12.350 --> 00:59:13.350
posts.

00:59:13.350 --> 00:59:20.960
Let's just say, you are Brian Armstrong, CEO
of Coinbase, and the company is doing well

00:59:20.960 --> 00:59:27.349
and you want to remove, and I don't want to
speak for him, but this has been very well

00:59:27.349 --> 00:59:28.349
covered.

00:59:28.349 --> 00:59:31.600
You want to put out a letter that effectively
says, "This is our mission.

00:59:31.600 --> 00:59:33.660
If you're aligned with this mission, great.

00:59:33.660 --> 00:59:41.230
But we cannot be responsible for every cause
or world event that pulls our direction at

00:59:41.230 --> 00:59:45.980
a given velocity towards whatever outcome
you might want.

00:59:45.980 --> 00:59:50.320
Therefore, if you would like to discuss these
things and feel like it should be done at

00:59:50.320 --> 00:59:51.839
work —
Jonathan Haidt: Go elsewhere.

00:59:51.839 --> 00:59:55.140
Tim Ferriss: — we're going to give you a
fantastic severance package."

00:59:55.140 --> 00:59:59.500
Part of the reason he can do that, aside from,
I think, being very courageous, thinking for

00:59:59.500 --> 01:00:04.280
himself, and being willing to take a lot of
flak in the short term, in the long-term best

01:00:04.280 --> 01:00:11.990
interest of the company, putting that aside,
he's also, I think, in doing that, unlikely

01:00:11.990 --> 01:00:12.990
to get fired.

01:00:12.990 --> 01:00:17.250
It would take the board of directors to remove
him and then there's the question of, "Well,

01:00:17.250 --> 01:00:22.920
who would we replace him with who would actually
be a good enough operator to do a better job?

01:00:22.920 --> 01:00:24.000
Going to be very tough."

01:00:24.000 --> 01:00:29.039
I suppose what I'm wondering is, within academics,
do you think there's anything that can be

01:00:29.039 --> 01:00:36.520
done to provide protections or safety nets
such that more people can actually take a

01:00:36.520 --> 01:00:37.520
counterposition?

01:00:37.520 --> 01:00:39.069
Jonathan Haidt: Yeah.

01:00:39.069 --> 01:00:40.069
Yes.

01:00:40.069 --> 01:00:47.640
Let's start by understanding the complex dynamical
system of a company in a capitalist economy.

01:00:47.640 --> 01:00:52.890
All of the stuff that started on campus in
2015, Greg and I wrote about it in The Coddling

01:00:52.890 --> 01:00:53.890
of the American Mind.

01:00:53.890 --> 01:00:58.370
We were going to have a chapter on the coddling
goes to work, but there weren't enough stories

01:00:58.370 --> 01:01:02.099
in 2017 when we were finishing the writing,
so we closed down the book.

01:01:02.099 --> 01:01:06.230
Then in 2018 it all starts blowing up, all
this stuff because Gen Z graduates, they move

01:01:06.230 --> 01:01:10.660
into the corporate world and they do the same
stuff there that they were doing on campus.

01:01:10.660 --> 01:01:13.869
The accusations that, "Someone wore a hat
that offended me.

01:01:13.869 --> 01:01:16.130
You have to punish them.

01:01:16.130 --> 01:01:17.960
This is an HR issue."

01:01:17.960 --> 01:01:21.640
So all that stuff starts around 2018 and 2019.

01:01:21.640 --> 01:01:27.059
Then, of course, in the summer of George Floyd,
the political activism intensifies for, again,

01:01:27.059 --> 01:01:31.980
understandable reasons, but in that summer,
almost all businesses, it seems, put out a

01:01:31.980 --> 01:01:33.340
statement on Black Lives Matter.

01:01:33.340 --> 01:01:38.410
Now, that was an exceptional summer, exceptional
event, but that then led to pressures that

01:01:38.410 --> 01:01:43.970
then led to a reorientation of much of corporate
America, at least in Progressively oriented,

01:01:43.970 --> 01:01:48.500
Progressive-leaning companies, much like what
happened to us in the academy in 2015, 2016.

01:01:48.500 --> 01:01:50.420
It was very, very similar.

01:01:50.420 --> 01:01:54.530
But the difference is that companies actually
have to make money.

01:01:54.530 --> 01:01:57.119
They actually have to be tied to the real
world.

01:01:57.119 --> 01:02:02.290
So if they do things that backfire, that cost
a lot of money without any benefit, like mandatory

01:02:02.290 --> 01:02:04.930
diversity training, it takes a lot of money.

01:02:04.930 --> 01:02:07.840
There's not really any evidence that makes
anything better.

01:02:07.840 --> 01:02:12.819
At some point, reality hits companies, whereas
universities are different.

01:02:12.819 --> 01:02:13.890
They're not like that.

01:02:13.890 --> 01:02:15.829
There is no reality.

01:02:15.829 --> 01:02:18.020
The leading ones have gigantic, well, they
have gigantic endowments.

01:02:18.020 --> 01:02:23.100
No matter how bad the service is, parents
all over the world want their kids to go to

01:02:23.100 --> 01:02:25.500
the top 10 or 15 American universities.

01:02:25.500 --> 01:02:31.039
It doesn't matter what happens, the product
can suck, but the demand for it is infinite.

01:02:31.039 --> 01:02:33.720
So universities are not tied to reality in
the way that a business is.

01:02:33.720 --> 01:02:34.829
That's the first thing.

01:02:34.829 --> 01:02:39.680
Second thing is that most businesses are not
as uniform as universities.

01:02:39.680 --> 01:02:42.480
Of course, Armstrong, I presume he's Right
of Center or Libertarian.

01:02:42.480 --> 01:02:43.480
Do you know?

01:02:43.480 --> 01:02:44.580
Tim Ferriss: I don't want to speak for him.

01:02:44.580 --> 01:02:45.580
I'm not sure.

01:02:45.580 --> 01:02:46.580
Jonathan Haidt: Is he Conservative?

01:02:46.580 --> 01:02:47.580
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I honestly have no idea.

01:02:47.580 --> 01:02:48.580
Jonathan Haidt: Okay.

01:02:48.580 --> 01:02:49.580
But in any — yeah.

01:02:49.580 --> 01:02:50.580
But I don't think he's Progressive.

01:02:50.580 --> 01:02:51.580
I think he was — so my point is that a number
of Right-leaning CEOs did that, and he was

01:02:51.580 --> 01:02:52.580
the first big one.

01:02:52.580 --> 01:02:53.920
And it's called the mission focus movement.

01:02:53.920 --> 01:02:55.040
That, "You know what?

01:02:55.040 --> 01:02:58.529
Our company is not here to fix everything
in the world.

01:02:58.529 --> 01:03:00.910
We're here to provide a great service for
our customers."

01:03:00.910 --> 01:03:02.529
So mission-focused.

01:03:02.529 --> 01:03:04.520
And a few right-leaning companies did that.

01:03:04.520 --> 01:03:07.660
And the one that really changed things then
was when Netflix did it.

01:03:07.660 --> 01:03:12.029
Because Netflix is a very Progressive company,
hugely important culturally.

01:03:12.029 --> 01:03:16.349
And when the Netflix CEO said something similar,
which was, "If your values mean that you can't

01:03:16.349 --> 01:03:21.440
work on certain projects, maybe you shouldn't
be working at Netflix."

01:03:21.440 --> 01:03:25.170
And I speak to a lot of managers and CEOs
and leaders and like, "Ugh."

01:03:25.170 --> 01:03:29.380
They were so thrilled because almost all leaders
hate this woke stuff.

01:03:29.380 --> 01:03:30.900
Almost all university presidents hate it.

01:03:30.900 --> 01:03:32.690
Makes their lives hell.

01:03:32.690 --> 01:03:38.460
So in the business world, they're able to
push back, and they have been.

01:03:38.460 --> 01:03:40.710
So the pendulum is swinging back in business.

01:03:40.710 --> 01:03:44.640
I don't yet see a sign on campus of a pendulum
swinging back.

01:03:44.640 --> 01:03:47.059
I think it will happen eventually.

01:03:47.059 --> 01:03:52.480
The complex dynamical system is much more
conducive to a kind of crazy ideological thing

01:03:52.480 --> 01:03:56.050
divorced from reality on campus.

01:03:56.050 --> 01:04:01.320
But the way it's going to break, I believe,
is things get sort of tense enough, unpleasant

01:04:01.320 --> 01:04:05.500
enough, wasteful enough, and then some school
is going to break.

01:04:05.500 --> 01:04:09.780
And I originally thought Chicago was the school,
and lots of schools would follow.

01:04:09.780 --> 01:04:10.780
And they didn't.

01:04:10.780 --> 01:04:11.780
Nobody followed Chicago.

01:04:11.780 --> 01:04:14.740
I mean, Mitch Daniels at Purdue, but that's
a different story.

01:04:14.740 --> 01:04:16.270
He's a Conservative.

01:04:16.270 --> 01:04:19.119
So I'm on the advisory board of the University
of Austin.

01:04:19.119 --> 01:04:20.859
Austin, the new — are you involved with
it at all?

01:04:20.859 --> 01:04:21.880
It's in your hometown there.

01:04:21.880 --> 01:04:24.520
Tim Ferriss: I'm not involved, but I have
some passing familiarity.

01:04:24.520 --> 01:04:26.559
For people who don't have any familiarity,
can you just explain what it is?

01:04:26.559 --> 01:04:28.010
Jonathan Haidt: Mm-hmm.

01:04:28.010 --> 01:04:34.279
Yeah, so it's a university founded on the
idea of we're going to rethink what a university

01:04:34.279 --> 01:04:35.540
should be.

01:04:35.540 --> 01:04:37.390
And Pano Kanelos is the president.

01:04:37.390 --> 01:04:39.670
He was the president of St. John's College
in Maryland.

01:04:39.670 --> 01:04:42.180
And so it's going to be focused on — it's
not going to have all the administrators,

01:04:42.180 --> 01:04:43.240
all the bells and whistles.

01:04:43.240 --> 01:04:47.200
It's going to really be focused on sort of
a classics education at the beginning, but

01:04:47.200 --> 01:04:49.170
then practical as you move on.

01:04:49.170 --> 01:04:54.859
So a lot of not just take a course, get a
credit for sitting in a seat, but do things

01:04:54.859 --> 01:04:56.990
as part of a team to solve a problem.

01:04:56.990 --> 01:05:02.950
So it's going to be given that higher ed now
is so expensive and is not as good as it used

01:05:02.950 --> 01:05:03.950
to be.

01:05:03.950 --> 01:05:04.950
The product is just not as good.

01:05:04.950 --> 01:05:05.950
It's not as much fun.

01:05:05.950 --> 01:05:07.539
It's not as rigorous.

01:05:07.539 --> 01:05:11.059
So given that the product is declining while
the price goes up, Austin is developing a

01:05:11.059 --> 01:05:13.309
model where the price is much, much lower.

01:05:13.309 --> 01:05:15.020
The product is a hell of a lot of fun.

01:05:15.020 --> 01:05:21.029
It's very exciting, and now it's just one
university.

01:05:21.029 --> 01:05:26.890
But the idea is it's going to be a model for
a lot of other universities to either change

01:05:26.890 --> 01:05:30.900
or be founded because I think we do need disruption
and innovation.

01:05:30.900 --> 01:05:32.849
We need alternatives.

01:05:32.849 --> 01:05:37.270
We can't just have the one sort of Ivy League
model of top universities, and they're all

01:05:37.270 --> 01:05:38.839
making the same mistakes at the same time.

01:05:38.839 --> 01:05:44.849
Tim Ferriss: I have a number of questions
that float to the surface, and I apologize

01:05:44.849 --> 01:05:49.260
for what is going to be a very inelegant way
of posing them.

01:05:49.260 --> 01:05:52.270
But I'm going to give you a menu of options,
and I'd love for you to choose whichever is

01:05:52.270 --> 01:05:54.079
of the greatest interest.

01:05:54.079 --> 01:06:03.029
So something that came to mind was how you
have preserved your ability or ensured your

01:06:03.029 --> 01:06:11.010
ability to speak very openly about these things
by maybe diversifying your identity and professional

01:06:11.010 --> 01:06:12.010
career.

01:06:12.010 --> 01:06:15.920
So not just teaching, but also books and so
on.

01:06:15.920 --> 01:06:16.920
Speaking, I assume.

01:06:16.920 --> 01:06:22.070
So you have a number of backup parachutes,
I would say.

01:06:22.070 --> 01:06:23.100
Perhaps in a sense.

01:06:23.100 --> 01:06:25.440
So maybe that's something to talk about.

01:06:25.440 --> 01:06:30.690
But what I wanted to ask you, specifically
related to University of Austin, because I

01:06:30.690 --> 01:06:35.080
did do a bit of reading related to it in prep
for the Niall Ferguson conversation.

01:06:35.080 --> 01:06:36.080
Jonathan Haidt: Mm-hmm.

01:06:36.080 --> 01:06:40.539
Tim Ferriss: It seems like, and I don't know
if this coverage is accurate, but that some

01:06:40.539 --> 01:06:44.119
people have start to finish strong supporters.

01:06:44.119 --> 01:06:48.230
There are and have been a number of very notable
names involved.

01:06:48.230 --> 01:06:49.780
So I encourage people to check it out.

01:06:49.780 --> 01:06:53.920
It seems like there have also been people
who agreed to be on advisory boards and so

01:06:53.920 --> 01:07:03.329
on who have folded or broken rank with public
criticism.

01:07:03.329 --> 01:07:10.450
How would you describe the reception and some
of those dynamics if what I'm reading is accurate?

01:07:10.450 --> 01:07:11.450
Jonathan Haidt: Mm-hmm.

01:07:11.450 --> 01:07:15.680
Well, we're in a space that is heavily politicized.

01:07:15.680 --> 01:07:20.000
There's a lot of attacks, almost all on Twitter
and social media.

01:07:20.000 --> 01:07:22.140
Most professors are reasonable.

01:07:22.140 --> 01:07:25.860
Most professors are Left, Center Left.

01:07:25.860 --> 01:07:27.150
They believe in free speech.

01:07:27.150 --> 01:07:30.619
They believe in that class we should talk
about ideas.

01:07:30.619 --> 01:07:32.579
So wherever you go, most people are reasonable.

01:07:32.579 --> 01:07:37.480
But the dynamics are such that if you stick
your neck out, this small fringe of academic

01:07:37.480 --> 01:07:42.559
Twitter is going to call you racist, sexist,
transphobe, whatever.

01:07:42.559 --> 01:07:45.420
And for a while, that was really scary.

01:07:45.420 --> 01:07:50.859
And I think now it's kind of like "The Boy
Who Cried Wolf" 10 million times once everyone's

01:07:50.859 --> 01:07:54.520
accused of racism, sexism, transphobia, Islamophobia.

01:07:54.520 --> 01:07:59.220
I think now people are just so exhausted that
I think now it's a little easier to stand

01:07:59.220 --> 01:08:01.350
that, that fringe on Twitter.

01:08:01.350 --> 01:08:09.970
When the University of Austin was announced,
I think there was a mistake in that some of

01:08:09.970 --> 01:08:14.039
the original material was a little bit pugnacious,
and it sounded sort of like, "This is going

01:08:14.039 --> 01:08:15.890
to be an anti-woke university."

01:08:15.890 --> 01:08:21.310
And once any particle is labeled like anti-woke,
now suddenly it's like, "Oh, now it's..."

01:08:21.310 --> 01:08:22.920
So I think there was a little brouhaha.

01:08:22.920 --> 01:08:26.920
I think that was a one fumble that the people
running it made in what was otherwise, I think,

01:08:26.920 --> 01:08:27.920
a very good launch.

01:08:27.920 --> 01:08:29.960
I mean, I think they really explained well
what it's going to be about.

01:08:29.960 --> 01:08:33.170
So I wish they hadn't done that because the
point — well, what I've learned from studying

01:08:33.170 --> 01:08:40.509
morality is, in a polarization spiral or a
culture war, the harder you hit your enemy,

01:08:40.509 --> 01:08:41.859
the stronger they get.

01:08:41.859 --> 01:08:43.790
And so you don't win by punching them really
hard.

01:08:43.790 --> 01:08:45.089
You can never destroy them.

01:08:45.089 --> 01:08:47.500
In a real war, you can literally kill them
and take their land.

01:08:47.500 --> 01:08:50.160
But in a culture war, you can't do that.

01:08:50.160 --> 01:08:51.830
And so I think it's really important.

01:08:51.830 --> 01:08:53.500
This is what we've done at Heterodox Academy.

01:08:53.500 --> 01:08:55.780
So I founded an organization called Heterodox
Academy.

01:08:55.780 --> 01:08:58.370
It's now run by John Tomasi as our president.

01:08:58.370 --> 01:09:02.230
And our attitude is we're not here to fight
the culture war.

01:09:02.230 --> 01:09:03.910
We're here to build great things.

01:09:03.910 --> 01:09:08.000
We're here to help people remember why they
love universities.

01:09:08.000 --> 01:09:09.120
Why universities are so important.

01:09:09.120 --> 01:09:13.830
And I think Austin is doing that.

01:09:13.830 --> 01:09:17.960
So I think, early on, it was a new thing,
and it wasn't clear what it was.

01:09:17.960 --> 01:09:22.120
And there were one or two people who left
the advisory board at that early stage.

01:09:22.120 --> 01:09:23.719
But I think now it's settled in.

01:09:23.719 --> 01:09:26.330
It's being successful.

01:09:26.330 --> 01:09:29.819
It doesn't yet have a full year of — had
a summer program.

01:09:29.819 --> 01:09:30.989
And I think we need variation.

01:09:30.989 --> 01:09:32.659
We need innovation.

01:09:32.659 --> 01:09:34.429
And so I'm actually very excited about it.

01:09:34.429 --> 01:09:35.480
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I agree.

01:09:35.480 --> 01:09:36.480
I'm excited to watch it.

01:09:36.480 --> 01:09:38.230
Could you say a bit more about Heterodox Academy?

01:09:38.230 --> 01:09:40.270
Jonathan Haidt: Oh, I'd love to.

01:09:40.270 --> 01:09:42.250
So I gave a talk in 2011.

01:09:42.250 --> 01:09:46.771
I was invited by the president of the Society
for Personality and Social Psychology to be

01:09:46.771 --> 01:09:50.750
part of a panel at our annual conference on
the future of social psychology.

01:09:50.750 --> 01:09:53.150
And we talked about my stepping out in 1993.

01:09:53.150 --> 01:09:54.950
I'm not on a team.

01:09:54.950 --> 01:09:56.330
I was still very much a Democrat.

01:09:56.330 --> 01:09:57.630
I voted for Democrats.

01:09:57.630 --> 01:10:00.980
By the time I was writing The Righteous Mind,
I began to really take seriously that you

01:10:00.980 --> 01:10:03.090
need viewpoint diversity.

01:10:03.090 --> 01:10:04.940
You need Conservatives and Libertarians.

01:10:04.940 --> 01:10:07.739
You can't just have a bunch of Progressives
studying psychology.

01:10:07.739 --> 01:10:09.020
You get bad results.

01:10:09.020 --> 01:10:11.150
And so I gave a talk on how this is a problem.

01:10:11.150 --> 01:10:12.150
This is a problem.

01:10:12.150 --> 01:10:13.620
We don't have any Conservatives.

01:10:13.620 --> 01:10:17.430
And I went through all kinds of methods of
finding a Conservative social psychologist.

01:10:17.430 --> 01:10:18.570
I couldn't find any.

01:10:18.570 --> 01:10:19.600
And I finally found one.

01:10:19.600 --> 01:10:23.450
So I gave a talk on this, and I wasn't kicked
out of the field.

01:10:23.450 --> 01:10:25.470
People actually said, "Oh, you're right.

01:10:25.470 --> 01:10:28.100
Actually, this would help us if we had some
political diversity."

01:10:28.100 --> 01:10:29.750
So that was 2011.

01:10:29.750 --> 01:10:36.440
And this was just a problem about the faculty,
and some other professors who were there liked

01:10:36.440 --> 01:10:37.440
what I said.

01:10:37.440 --> 01:10:38.550
They said, "Yeah, we think this is a problem
too."

01:10:38.550 --> 01:10:42.760
So we wrote a paper together on laying it
out more formally, and I think the title was

01:10:42.760 --> 01:10:46.489
"Political Diversity Will Improve Social Psychological
Science."

01:10:46.489 --> 01:10:49.690
So this is a straight academic faculty project.

01:10:49.690 --> 01:10:53.690
And then it turns out, actually, the same
problem was happening in law schools and sociology.

01:10:53.690 --> 01:11:00.620
In a lot of fields, their research was not
reliable because of orthodoxy, political orthodoxy.

01:11:00.620 --> 01:11:03.900
So we all got together and said, "Hey, let's
put up a website."

01:11:03.900 --> 01:11:08.900
And Nick Rosenkrantz, a law professor at Georgetown,
came up with the name Heterodox Academy.

01:11:08.900 --> 01:11:11.200
It's the opposite of Orthodox Academy.

01:11:11.200 --> 01:11:14.560
Orthodox means straight, one — there's only
one way of thinking.

01:11:14.560 --> 01:11:16.270
Heterodox means there should be a variety
of ways of thinking.

01:11:16.270 --> 01:11:19.620
We need that in order to be successful.

01:11:19.620 --> 01:11:20.650
So we put up the website.

01:11:20.650 --> 01:11:26.230
We started it as a blog around September 12th
of 2015.

01:11:26.230 --> 01:11:29.910
And very soon, right after that, Yale blew
up, the universities blew up, and suddenly

01:11:29.910 --> 01:11:31.310
it was like, "Wait a second.

01:11:31.310 --> 01:11:32.810
We thought this was just a faculty thing.

01:11:32.810 --> 01:11:35.590
But now it turns out the campuses are going
insane.

01:11:35.590 --> 01:11:37.790
The students are behaving in this strange
way.

01:11:37.790 --> 01:11:41.810
They're saying everything is violence, everything
is danger, everything's a threat."

01:11:41.810 --> 01:11:47.489
So Heterodox Academy grew from just this faculty
project to really being much more about university

01:11:47.489 --> 01:11:48.489
culture.

01:11:48.489 --> 01:11:55.090
To do our jobs, we have to have not just viewpoint
diversity but a sense of grace, a sense of

01:11:55.090 --> 01:12:01.010
tolerance, a sense of intellectual humility,
and generosity.

01:12:01.010 --> 01:12:02.970
We have to be able to think together.

01:12:02.970 --> 01:12:06.180
We have to have people like Rick Shweder saying,
"Okay, you asserted that.

01:12:06.180 --> 01:12:07.460
Well, let me try the opposite."

01:12:07.460 --> 01:12:10.550
And then they don't get fired for doing it.

01:12:10.550 --> 01:12:12.060
So that's what we're trying to do.

01:12:12.060 --> 01:12:14.580
Heterodox Academy now has — we have a couple
of big projects.

01:12:14.580 --> 01:12:21.639
One is we're branching out and having campus
communities where we're going to be working

01:12:21.639 --> 01:12:24.989
directly on campus with our many — we have
about 5,000 members.

01:12:24.989 --> 01:12:30.960
And I invite everyone who's listening here,
if you are a professor or an administrator,

01:12:30.960 --> 01:12:35.430
if you're an insider to universities, if you're
part of the community that is tasked with

01:12:35.430 --> 01:12:41.380
preserving these precious institutions, please
go to heterodoxacademy.org and click on "About

01:12:41.380 --> 01:12:42.380
Us" and join.

01:12:42.380 --> 01:12:43.380
You can see our membership.

01:12:43.380 --> 01:12:44.380
It's free.

01:12:44.380 --> 01:12:51.080
We have 5,000 professors as members, and we're
equally divided, Left, Right, Libertarian.

01:12:51.080 --> 01:12:53.600
We're sort of all over the map.

01:12:53.600 --> 01:12:55.250
And it's a wonderful community.

01:12:55.250 --> 01:13:00.310
If you remember when it was fun, when there
were jokes and exciting ideas, if you remember

01:13:00.310 --> 01:13:06.020
when everything wasn't so dangerous, please
join us because that's what we're trying to

01:13:06.020 --> 01:13:07.020
recall and restore.

01:13:07.020 --> 01:13:13.560
Tim Ferriss: Who are some of the thinkers
who have informed how you think about intellectual

01:13:13.560 --> 01:13:15.680
heterodoxy?

01:13:15.680 --> 01:13:17.860
And I will throw one out there because I think
that —

01:13:17.860 --> 01:13:18.860
Jonathan Haidt: Okay.

01:13:18.860 --> 01:13:19.860
Tim Ferriss: — John Stuart Mill.

01:13:19.860 --> 01:13:20.860
Jonathan Haidt: Oh, yes.

01:13:20.860 --> 01:13:25.500
Tim Ferriss: And is this quote from John Stuart
Mill, "He who knows his own side of the case,

01:13:25.500 --> 01:13:26.770
knows little of that."

01:13:26.770 --> 01:13:28.620
Is that John Stuart —
Jonathan Haidt: "He who knows only his own

01:13:28.620 --> 01:13:29.620
side…"

01:13:29.620 --> 01:13:30.620
Tim Ferriss: Only.

01:13:30.620 --> 01:13:33.030
Jonathan Haidt: "...of the case — only his
own side of the case knows little of that."

01:13:33.030 --> 01:13:34.030
Exactly.

01:13:34.030 --> 01:13:35.030
That's right.

01:13:35.030 --> 01:13:38.230
So John Stuart Mill is the patron saint of
viewpoint diversity.

01:13:38.230 --> 01:13:39.350
In fact, I would urge — I should have a
copy of it here somewhere.

01:13:39.350 --> 01:13:43.130
One of the greatest works of the Liberal tradition
is On Liberty.

01:13:43.130 --> 01:13:45.730
It's a kind of a long book with dense prose.

01:13:45.730 --> 01:13:49.920
But chapter two is the key chapter on diversity
of thought or freedom of thought.

01:13:49.920 --> 01:13:51.050
Liberty of thought, I think it is.

01:13:51.050 --> 01:13:53.890
So at Heterodox Academy, we took that chapter.

01:13:53.890 --> 01:13:54.940
It's 15,000 words.

01:13:54.940 --> 01:13:56.380
We cut it in half.

01:13:56.380 --> 01:13:57.380
We edit it down.

01:13:57.380 --> 01:13:59.460
So you have a shortened version, much easier
to read.

01:13:59.460 --> 01:14:02.230
And it's beautifully illustrated by brilliant
artist Dave Cicirelli.

01:14:02.230 --> 01:14:07.860
So if you go to heterodoxacademy.org/mil.

01:14:07.860 --> 01:14:09.210
It's a free ebook.

01:14:09.210 --> 01:14:10.710
You can buy it on Amazon for $20.

01:14:10.710 --> 01:14:13.690
But we put out a free ebook because we want
students to read it.

01:14:13.690 --> 01:14:18.330
And I mean, it really just makes the case
that you need viewpoint diversity.

01:14:18.330 --> 01:14:22.060
If you want to get smart, if you want to figure
things out, an amazing thing happens.

01:14:22.060 --> 01:14:27.590
And this is what I've really taken to heart
from studying John Stuart Mill, is if you

01:14:27.590 --> 01:14:31.000
want to improve your thought, the world will
help you if you ask.

01:14:31.000 --> 01:14:34.810
And if you go to people who disagree with
you and say, "Here's what I'm thinking, where

01:14:34.810 --> 01:14:35.810
am I wrong?"

01:14:35.810 --> 01:14:36.810
They'll tell you.

01:14:36.810 --> 01:14:41.660
And so when Greg and I wrote The Coddling
of the American Mind, I paid seven social

01:14:41.660 --> 01:14:43.199
justice activists to read it.

01:14:43.199 --> 01:14:47.250
Paid them each $700, and they read it and
they gave me their comments and their criticisms,

01:14:47.250 --> 01:14:50.660
but one or two of them were kind of pissed
off or annoyed.

01:14:50.660 --> 01:14:53.790
But the others were actually like, "Oh, this
is interesting.

01:14:53.790 --> 01:14:54.850
We really engaged with each other."

01:14:54.850 --> 01:14:56.330
And they made the book better.

01:14:56.330 --> 01:14:58.480
So you really need to seek out criticism.

01:14:58.480 --> 01:15:02.750
You need to seek out people who differ from
you, and then, actually, you'll get smarter.

01:15:02.750 --> 01:15:12.840
Tim Ferriss: Have you found other people who
have studied morality to defuse or defang

01:15:12.840 --> 01:15:15.790
their anger in the same way that you experienced
it yourself?

01:15:15.790 --> 01:15:17.800
Is that a pattern that you've observed in
other people?

01:15:17.800 --> 01:15:18.800
Jonathan Haidt: What?

01:15:18.800 --> 01:15:21.150
The ability to defang the anger to get past
it?

01:15:21.150 --> 01:15:22.150
Tim Ferriss: Right.

01:15:22.150 --> 01:15:28.210
Well, your description of how you, I wouldn't
say ceased to be angry, but lost your anger

01:15:28.210 --> 01:15:29.750
in the course of studying morality.

01:15:29.750 --> 01:15:33.750
Is that something that you have seen replicated
in other people or is that —

01:15:33.750 --> 01:15:34.880
Jonathan Haidt: Oh, yes.

01:15:34.880 --> 01:15:35.880
Oh, yes.

01:15:35.880 --> 01:15:38.730
So, first of all, the Buddhists and the Stoics.

01:15:38.730 --> 01:15:42.390
These are the two most concentrated wisdom
traditions on earth, I believe.

01:15:42.390 --> 01:15:45.980
My first book, The Happiness Hypothesis, was
about — I read all the ancient wisdom I

01:15:45.980 --> 01:15:48.290
could find, took out all the psychological
claims.

01:15:48.290 --> 01:15:53.710
And so chapter four of that book is on the
ancient idea of, "Why do you see the speck

01:15:53.710 --> 01:15:56.429
in your neighbor's eye when you cannot see
the plank in your own?"

01:15:56.429 --> 01:15:57.630
We're all hypocrites.

01:15:57.630 --> 01:16:03.410
So following certain wisdom traditions, especially
Buddhism and Stoicism, but also Christianity,

01:16:03.410 --> 01:16:10.600
Judaism, you find these injunctions to go
past good and evil to be more tolerant.

01:16:10.600 --> 01:16:16.810
So this is just sort of the progression of
wisdom that most great cultures have realized.

01:16:16.810 --> 01:16:17.890
You also see it in a lot of people.

01:16:17.890 --> 01:16:23.210
I think as people get older — so at least
in men, our testosterone levels, they're really

01:16:23.210 --> 01:16:26.679
high in the teens, 20s, and then they sort
of go down steadily.

01:16:26.679 --> 01:16:31.290
And that steady drop, I think, it also mirrors
the drop in violent criminality.

01:16:31.290 --> 01:16:35.200
And I believe it also mirrors the drop in
aggressive rock music.

01:16:35.200 --> 01:16:40.540
I used to always need loud music, and as I
get older, I don't.

01:16:40.540 --> 01:16:42.520
But I think it's just — also, it's calming.

01:16:42.520 --> 01:16:46.540
So young men are more passionate and moralistic,
perhaps.

01:16:46.540 --> 01:16:48.960
And then, as you get older, you get kind of
calmer.

01:16:48.960 --> 01:16:51.570
But there are lots of paragons of this.

01:16:51.570 --> 01:16:54.639
Daryl Davis being, perhaps, one of the preeminent
ones.

01:16:54.639 --> 01:17:01.350
Daryl Davis, a Black musician, blues musician,
who was playing in a bar and ended up sitting

01:17:01.350 --> 01:17:04.440
next to a Ku Klux Klan member, and he's conversing
with the guy.

01:17:04.440 --> 01:17:08.020
And rather than getting upset or angry or
running away, he listens to him and listens

01:17:08.020 --> 01:17:12.710
to him, gets in a conversation with him, and
they develop a friendship.

01:17:12.710 --> 01:17:15.889
And he gets the guy to give up his robe and
leave the Klan.

01:17:15.889 --> 01:17:16.889
And he's done this hundreds of times.

01:17:16.889 --> 01:17:17.889
Tim Ferriss: Wow.

01:17:17.889 --> 01:17:18.889
How do you spell Daryl's name?

01:17:18.889 --> 01:17:19.889
Jonathan Haidt: Daryl Davis.

01:17:19.889 --> 01:17:20.889
Yeah.

01:17:20.889 --> 01:17:23.000
D-A-R-Y-L or is it double R?

01:17:23.000 --> 01:17:24.730
Daryl Davis is D-A-V-I-S.

01:17:24.730 --> 01:17:25.730
Tim Ferriss: Mm-hmm.

01:17:25.730 --> 01:17:27.960
Jonathan Haidt: He's got a one or two TED
Talks that are great, and he's got a book

01:17:27.960 --> 01:17:28.960
or two.

01:17:28.960 --> 01:17:36.150
So Daryl Davis, what an inspiring example
of how is your goal to impress everyone else

01:17:36.150 --> 01:17:37.150
with your righteous reaction.

01:17:37.150 --> 01:17:40.341
Well, if so, then you should get angry, and
you should yell and scream, and you should

01:17:40.341 --> 01:17:41.341
post things on Twitter.

01:17:41.341 --> 01:17:43.250
Or is your goal to actually change someone?

01:17:43.250 --> 01:17:47.790
And if your goal is to change someone, listen
to them, try to understand them.

01:17:47.790 --> 01:17:49.909
And if you do that, what Daryl says is, "You
listen to them like they've never been listened

01:17:49.909 --> 01:17:51.160
to before.

01:17:51.160 --> 01:17:52.570
And then an amazing thing happens.

01:17:52.570 --> 01:17:54.100
Then they'll listen to you."

01:17:54.100 --> 01:17:55.100
Tim Ferriss: Mm-hmm.

01:17:55.100 --> 01:17:57.450
Jonathan Haidt: And so that's really the magic.

01:17:57.450 --> 01:17:59.710
And this is also Dale Carnegie.

01:17:59.710 --> 01:18:05.199
This is so many wise people that the things
we do to show off our morality for others

01:18:05.199 --> 01:18:07.890
end up isolating us and making us stupid.

01:18:07.890 --> 01:18:09.460
Tim Ferriss: And unhappy, oftentimes, it would
seem.

01:18:09.460 --> 01:18:10.460
Jonathan Haidt: Unhappy.

01:18:10.460 --> 01:18:11.460
Yeah.

01:18:11.460 --> 01:18:12.460
Well, anger, shared anger, actually.

01:18:12.460 --> 01:18:13.460
So, anger in the brain is actually an approach.

01:18:13.460 --> 01:18:14.460
Anger is not a negative emotion.

01:18:14.460 --> 01:18:18.230
Anger in the brain, it looks more like a positive
emotion because a shared anger is thrilling,

01:18:18.230 --> 01:18:20.880
and that's part of what's driving us off a
cliff as a country.

01:18:20.880 --> 01:18:30.810
Tim Ferriss: What would you say are some of
the conclusions or observations made in The

01:18:30.810 --> 01:18:37.190
Happiness Hypothesis that you revisit most
often or underscore for yourself?

01:18:37.190 --> 01:18:39.620
And what is your set point, if you have one?

01:18:39.620 --> 01:18:40.679
Jonathan Haidt: Let's see, what's my set point?

01:18:40.679 --> 01:18:41.679
Yeah.

01:18:41.679 --> 01:18:43.380
So the set point question is easy.

01:18:43.380 --> 01:18:46.210
That just refers to the fact that happiness
is heritable.

01:18:46.210 --> 01:18:49.290
And identical twins separated at birth will
tend to be similarly happy.

01:18:49.290 --> 01:18:51.179
And my set point's sort of right in the middle.

01:18:51.179 --> 01:18:55.909
I'm not born — I'm generally optimistic,
but I've been depressed at a couple of points

01:18:55.909 --> 01:18:56.909
in my life.

01:18:56.909 --> 01:19:00.000
I'm not very prone to it, but I've had some.

01:19:00.000 --> 01:19:02.260
And so I'm sort of in the middle.

01:19:02.260 --> 01:19:05.960
But the level of happiness that you live at
is not your set point.

01:19:05.960 --> 01:19:13.040
Where you live is sort of a range around your
set point, depending on a few conditions.

01:19:13.040 --> 01:19:17.540
And those conditions are basically love and
work, as Sigmund Freud said.

01:19:17.540 --> 01:19:18.540
Love and work.

01:19:18.540 --> 01:19:21.850
Those are — if you're doing well in those,
you'll be at the top end of your range.

01:19:21.850 --> 01:19:28.360
So I teach a course at NYU, a graduate seminar
called "Work, Wisdom, and Happiness."

01:19:28.360 --> 01:19:29.710
And it's a positive psychology class.

01:19:29.710 --> 01:19:33.190
But in the last year, I've sort of readjusted
the syllabus.

01:19:33.190 --> 01:19:39.960
I've rewired the course so that the title
could be "Smarter, Stronger, More Sociable."

01:19:39.960 --> 01:19:42.190
And so we have sections on how do you get
smarter.

01:19:42.190 --> 01:19:45.650
And you do that — it turns out the big thing
you do, turn off your notifications, get your

01:19:45.650 --> 01:19:46.730
attention back.

01:19:46.730 --> 01:19:49.480
Read Johann Hari's book Stolen Focus.

01:19:49.480 --> 01:19:54.480
So you can make yourself 10 or 15 IQ points
smarter if you can regain control of your

01:19:54.480 --> 01:19:56.790
attention and cut down on the moralism.

01:19:56.790 --> 01:19:57.790
Those are the two big steps.

01:19:57.790 --> 01:19:59.290
So how do you get smarter?

01:19:59.290 --> 01:20:01.830
How do you get stronger?

01:20:01.830 --> 01:20:06.570
That's The Coddling of the American Mind,
Antifragility, Nassim Taleb.

01:20:06.570 --> 01:20:11.500
You get stronger by challenging yourself,
by exposing yourself to threats and dangers

01:20:11.500 --> 01:20:16.400
within limits that you then surmount, and
we have to do this with kids.

01:20:16.400 --> 01:20:17.400
And how do you get more sociable?

01:20:17.400 --> 01:20:22.670
Well, it's actually some of the same skills,
but the main one is really listen to people.

01:20:22.670 --> 01:20:25.380
And you can't do that as long as you're always
distracted, as long as you're always on your

01:20:25.380 --> 01:20:26.480
phone.

01:20:26.480 --> 01:20:28.550
So there are some really easy things you can
do.

01:20:28.550 --> 01:20:31.270
And then the students learn to do some harder
things.

01:20:31.270 --> 01:20:38.110
But if they can get smarter, stronger, and
more sociable, then they will be more successful

01:20:38.110 --> 01:20:40.250
at work and in relationships.

01:20:40.250 --> 01:20:44.160
And if they're more successful in work and
relationships, then I promise them on the

01:20:44.160 --> 01:20:48.960
first day, then they will be happier because
that's how you live at the high end of your

01:20:48.960 --> 01:20:52.550
set range is by having your love and your
work go well.

01:20:52.550 --> 01:20:58.860
Tim Ferriss: Is this course in any capacity
available online or could it be made available

01:20:58.860 --> 01:21:00.710
online?

01:21:00.710 --> 01:21:01.710
Any aspect?

01:21:01.710 --> 01:21:03.179
Maybe a reading list?

01:21:03.179 --> 01:21:04.179
Maybe questions?

01:21:04.179 --> 01:21:06.560
Jonathan Haidt: Well, the syllabus is online.

01:21:06.560 --> 01:21:10.710
And you know what, I should do something like
— so right now — so it was just a seminar

01:21:10.710 --> 01:21:16.460
at Stern with 35 MBA students, twice — two
sections a year.

01:21:16.460 --> 01:21:20.520
And I've just raised it to 55 students while
teaching more sections.

01:21:20.520 --> 01:21:23.320
I should put something up.

01:21:23.320 --> 01:21:24.320
You know what?

01:21:24.320 --> 01:21:25.320
I'm going to start a Substack.

01:21:25.320 --> 01:21:27.080
Oh, my God, this is great.

01:21:27.080 --> 01:21:29.230
I'm actually going to start a Substack in
January.

01:21:29.230 --> 01:21:31.400
I can't believe I have — I didn't even think
of this.

01:21:31.400 --> 01:21:33.610
The Substack people would kill me for not
announcing this.

01:21:33.610 --> 01:21:34.610
Yeah.

01:21:34.610 --> 01:21:40.500
So it's going to be called After Babbel is
the name of the Substack, and I can just put

01:21:40.500 --> 01:21:41.810
up whatever I want.

01:21:41.810 --> 01:21:44.580
I used to blog at righteousmind.com, but that
was not much.

01:21:44.580 --> 01:21:48.130
So I'm going to start blogging, and I thank
you for that, Tim.

01:21:48.130 --> 01:21:52.660
I will put some — whatever I can put easily
online or in there.

01:21:52.660 --> 01:21:54.960
I will put there, at least, the reading list
and the syllabus.

01:21:54.960 --> 01:21:55.960
Tim Ferriss: Amazing.

01:21:55.960 --> 01:21:59.060
Jonathan Haidt: And eventually, maybe, I could
make — I could tape the lectures or do something

01:21:59.060 --> 01:22:01.860
to put — yeah, I'll think about it.

01:22:01.860 --> 01:22:06.460
Tim Ferriss: Is there a department and number
for the class?

01:22:06.460 --> 01:22:11.080
Something that people could look for online?

01:22:11.080 --> 01:22:14.620
I'm not sure how that would work in MBA programs,
but I'm just thinking.

01:22:14.620 --> 01:22:17.820
The class that I used to visit at Princeton
was ELE 491, right?

01:22:17.820 --> 01:22:18.820
High-tech entrepreneurship.

01:22:18.820 --> 01:22:19.820
Jonathan Haidt: Right.

01:22:19.820 --> 01:22:20.820
Tim Ferriss: So it made it easy to find.

01:22:20.820 --> 01:22:21.820
Jonathan Haidt: Oh, yeah.

01:22:21.820 --> 01:22:23.070
There's no point in them finding it because
it's —

01:22:23.070 --> 01:22:24.070
Tim Ferriss: There's nothing.

01:22:24.070 --> 01:22:25.360
Jonathan Haidt: — it fills up with Stern
students.

01:22:25.360 --> 01:22:27.460
Even if you're at NYU but not at Stern, you
can't take it.

01:22:27.460 --> 01:22:28.460
But I will.

01:22:28.460 --> 01:22:30.700
But if you just go to — so all my stuff
is at jonathanhaidt.com.

01:22:30.700 --> 01:22:34.790
I will find a way to put it on my site, and
then I will — I think our discussion, it

01:22:34.790 --> 01:22:38.480
might air before New Year's, but the Substack
isn't going to be up until late January, probably.

01:22:38.480 --> 01:22:40.220
But just I'll do something.

01:22:40.220 --> 01:22:41.220
Thank you.

01:22:41.220 --> 01:22:44.860
Tim Ferriss: And it's easy for us to put in
the show notes, so once anything is available

01:22:44.860 --> 01:22:45.860
—
Jonathan Haidt: Oh, yeah, okay.

01:22:45.860 --> 01:22:48.390
Tim Ferriss: — we can add it to the show
notes at tim.blog/podcast, and people can

01:22:48.390 --> 01:22:54.000
find you by just searching H-A-I-D-T.

01:22:54.000 --> 01:22:58.670
Let's talk about religion and happiness.

01:22:58.670 --> 01:23:03.250
So I have observed, I'm not the only one.

01:23:03.250 --> 01:23:14.850
Generally speaking, I would say the, let's
just say, self-described, rational, materialist,

01:23:14.850 --> 01:23:20.290
Left-leaning folks to be pretty unhappy.

01:23:20.290 --> 01:23:21.659
That is a — overall.

01:23:21.659 --> 01:23:22.700
Jonathan Haidt: Yeah.

01:23:22.700 --> 01:23:30.850
Tim Ferriss: And I have found very smart,
very thoughtful people who would identify

01:23:30.850 --> 01:23:36.640
as religious to on average be quite a bit
happier.

01:23:36.640 --> 01:23:47.570
And I know happy can be a slippery term, but
if we're looking at work and love, work and

01:23:47.570 --> 01:23:51.500
relationships, they seem to check a lot of
boxes very well.

01:23:51.500 --> 01:23:58.250
But it seems to me, and I'm just going to
speak for a second here, that with religion,

01:23:58.250 --> 01:24:09.239
you also remove a lot of uncertainty, decision
fatigue, paradox of choice that makes life

01:24:09.239 --> 01:24:12.080
less of a death by a thousand paper cuts.

01:24:12.080 --> 01:24:17.429
And maybe that also contributes to general
sense of well-being, that many of your decisions

01:24:17.429 --> 01:24:21.659
are more automated, perhaps people you spend
time with, perhaps your weekly structure,

01:24:21.659 --> 01:24:24.260
the architecture of your life.

01:24:24.260 --> 01:24:27.120
How do you think about this?

01:24:27.120 --> 01:24:35.070
What have you seen in your observations and
studies with respect to religion and happiness?

01:24:35.070 --> 01:24:37.710
And are there ingredients from religion —
Jonathan Haidt: Yes.

01:24:37.710 --> 01:24:41.840
Tim Ferriss: — that secular folks can borrow
to get some of that upside?

01:24:41.840 --> 01:24:43.139
Jonathan Haidt: Yes.

01:24:43.139 --> 01:24:46.330
So there's a huge amount of research on happiness
and who's happy.

01:24:46.330 --> 01:24:52.090
And the two big things that really come out
consistently are religious people are happier

01:24:52.090 --> 01:24:54.480
and married people are happier.

01:24:54.480 --> 01:25:00.210
Religion and marriage are the two giant demographic
factors or quasi-demographic factors that

01:25:00.210 --> 01:25:02.409
seem to go with happiness.

01:25:02.409 --> 01:25:04.000
And then there's a lot of question as to why.

01:25:04.000 --> 01:25:05.310
Why does religion make people happier?

01:25:05.310 --> 01:25:06.440
It might be what you said.

01:25:06.440 --> 01:25:08.409
You don't have to think about things.

01:25:08.409 --> 01:25:09.590
You have a sense of meaning.

01:25:09.590 --> 01:25:11.510
You don't worry about the afterlife, whatever
it is.

01:25:11.510 --> 01:25:17.030
No, it turns out when they look, you do empirical
studies of what elements of religious people's

01:25:17.030 --> 01:25:19.940
lives correlate with happiness.

01:25:19.940 --> 01:25:22.989
Overwhelmingly, it's nothing about belief.

01:25:22.989 --> 01:25:28.060
It's participation in a community on a weekly
basis, especially a community that has some

01:25:28.060 --> 01:25:33.000
sort of moral principle, some sense of uplifting,
some sense of self-control, binding moral

01:25:33.000 --> 01:25:34.000
responsibility.

01:25:34.000 --> 01:25:36.719
And so, actually, this allows me to go back
to Durkheim.

01:25:36.719 --> 01:25:40.800
I dropped that name in before but didn't really
tell listeners anything about him.

01:25:40.800 --> 01:25:45.320
So Émile Durkheim is one of the most important
sociologists in history.

01:25:45.320 --> 01:25:50.890
He's writing in France in the 1890s and early
1900s.

01:25:50.890 --> 01:25:54.010
And one of his early works is this incredible
study of suicide.

01:25:54.010 --> 01:25:58.260
Now, this is when European countries are just
beginning to collect statistics.

01:25:58.260 --> 01:26:03.270
And he goes through all the data he can get
on suicide in different countries in Europe.

01:26:03.270 --> 01:26:06.949
And he is looking for what makes the suicide
rate go up and down.

01:26:06.949 --> 01:26:09.400
And he treats the suicide rate as a thing
in itself.

01:26:09.400 --> 01:26:10.889
It's a sociological fact.

01:26:10.889 --> 01:26:12.290
It's an emergent fact about a country.

01:26:12.290 --> 01:26:13.660
Its rate goes up and down.

01:26:13.660 --> 01:26:15.750
What makes it go up and down?

01:26:15.750 --> 01:26:19.670
Well, when you go to war, the suicide rate
goes way down.

01:26:19.670 --> 01:26:20.929
People are bound together.

01:26:20.929 --> 01:26:22.210
They don't kill themselves.

01:26:22.210 --> 01:26:26.730
When you look at who in a country kills themselves,
it turns out if you're married, you're less

01:26:26.730 --> 01:26:27.730
likely to.

01:26:27.730 --> 01:26:31.140
If you're married with children, you're even
less likely to.

01:26:31.140 --> 01:26:35.080
Whereas if you're Protestant, you're more
likely to, but if you're Catholic or Jewish,

01:26:35.080 --> 01:26:36.820
you're less likely to.

01:26:36.820 --> 01:26:42.290
And over and over, what he finds is the degree
to which people are bound into a society,

01:26:42.290 --> 01:26:44.130
the less likely they are to kill themselves.

01:26:44.130 --> 01:26:47.880
Now, in East Asian cultures, in many cultures,
you kill yourself because you're too tightly

01:26:47.880 --> 01:26:49.590
bound and you're ashamed.

01:26:49.590 --> 01:26:53.090
But in Western cultures, our problem is anomie
or normlessness.

01:26:53.090 --> 01:26:55.080
You have a sense of meaninglessness.

01:26:55.080 --> 01:26:57.280
You're not connected.

01:26:57.280 --> 01:27:00.160
That's why Americans and Westerners kill themselves
mostly.

01:27:00.160 --> 01:27:05.010
And so, back to religion.

01:27:05.010 --> 01:27:09.969
If you are an atheist and you're not part
of a community, and especially if you're a

01:27:09.969 --> 01:27:11.610
new atheist and you're angry or whatever.

01:27:11.610 --> 01:27:16.469
But the protective factor seems to be being
bound into relationships and community.

01:27:16.469 --> 01:27:18.810
And that fits with everything else I know
in psychology.

01:27:18.810 --> 01:27:26.889
Tim Ferriss: The comment on wartime also makes
me think about Tribe by Sebastian Junger,

01:27:26.889 --> 01:27:28.110
which I —
Jonathan Haidt: Oh, yeah.

01:27:28.110 --> 01:27:30.410
Tim Ferriss: — highly recommend to people.

01:27:30.410 --> 01:27:41.869
It really underscores the fragility of isolation
and also the fragility of fetishizing comfort

01:27:41.869 --> 01:27:43.080
in a way.

01:27:43.080 --> 01:27:48.080
I suggest people take a look at that.

01:27:48.080 --> 01:27:53.830
Well, Jonathan, we've covered a lot here,
and I could ask many more questions, but what

01:27:53.830 --> 01:27:55.280
have I neglected to ask?

01:27:55.280 --> 01:28:00.330
And I do have a question about heresies of
yours in a moment, but is there anything that

01:28:00.330 --> 01:28:03.219
you would like to bring up that I have not
mentioned?

01:28:03.219 --> 01:28:04.480
Jonathan Haidt: Yes.

01:28:04.480 --> 01:28:06.860
So haven't talked about what's happening to
Gen Z.

01:28:06.860 --> 01:28:08.800
We haven't talked about what's happening to
kids.

01:28:08.800 --> 01:28:14.060
I talked about them before in a sort of negative
way, like Gen Z shows up on campus, and then

01:28:14.060 --> 01:28:16.730
all of a sudden, there are all these problems.

01:28:16.730 --> 01:28:18.310
But again, I don't get angry.

01:28:18.310 --> 01:28:19.310
I'm not moralistic.

01:28:19.310 --> 01:28:22.270
We have to understand what's happening to
Gen Z.

01:28:22.270 --> 01:28:23.970
So I just want to talk, if I can, just talk
a little bit about that.

01:28:23.970 --> 01:28:24.970
Tim Ferriss: Please.

01:28:24.970 --> 01:28:27.850
And could also define Gen Z?

01:28:27.850 --> 01:28:29.920
And then please continue.

01:28:29.920 --> 01:28:30.920
Jonathan Haidt: Yeah.

01:28:30.920 --> 01:28:35.700
So Millennials are those who were born in
1982, and we used to think that it would go

01:28:35.700 --> 01:28:37.480
to maybe 2000.

01:28:37.480 --> 01:28:43.890
But it turns out that kids who were born in
1996 and later are different from those born,

01:28:43.890 --> 01:28:45.020
say, 1994 and earlier.

01:28:45.020 --> 01:28:50.300
I mean, it's a surprisingly sharp-cut equivalent
to what we find with birth here in 1946.

01:28:50.300 --> 01:28:52.980
The post-war world really was different to
grow up in.

01:28:52.980 --> 01:28:57.310
And I believe — so I'm drawing on work by
Jean Twenge, but I've also added — she and

01:28:57.310 --> 01:29:00.460
I are working together, and I've added a lot.

01:29:00.460 --> 01:29:06.860
What happened is that if you were born in,
say, 1990, you didn't get an iPhone until

01:29:06.860 --> 01:29:10.580
maybe around 2009, '10 is when teenagers started
getting it.

01:29:10.580 --> 01:29:16.030
So if you're born in 1990, you didn't get
an iPhone, a smartphone until you were 20.

01:29:16.030 --> 01:29:17.760
And you didn't get on social media.

01:29:17.760 --> 01:29:21.601
You might've been on Facebook partly, but
you don't live on social media until you have

01:29:21.601 --> 01:29:23.400
your own smartphone.

01:29:23.400 --> 01:29:31.700
Whereas if you're born in 1997, you are 13
in 2010 when you might get your first smartphone,

01:29:31.700 --> 01:29:36.980
and you might get on Instagram in 2012 when
huge numbers did.

01:29:36.980 --> 01:29:41.159
So I believe Gen Z is defined by the fact
that they got smartphones and social media

01:29:41.159 --> 01:29:42.300
during early puberty.

01:29:42.300 --> 01:29:47.199
There's a lot of research pointing to early
puberty, around 11 to 13 for girls, 12 to

01:29:47.199 --> 01:29:48.860
15 for boys.

01:29:48.860 --> 01:29:52.199
What's coming into your brain then is really
important because your frontal cortex is wiring

01:29:52.199 --> 01:29:53.199
itself up.

01:29:53.199 --> 01:29:57.670
And so when human beings are raised without
much independence, but yes, with a phone,

01:29:57.670 --> 01:30:02.659
and they spend their childhood just interacting
with a screen, and especially social media,

01:30:02.659 --> 01:30:06.650
I believe it messes up cortical development.

01:30:06.650 --> 01:30:11.500
The girls, in particular, the rates of anxiety
and depression are up much more than 100 percent

01:30:11.500 --> 01:30:12.710
since 2010.

01:30:12.710 --> 01:30:18.730
The rate of self-harm, hospitalization for
self-harm is nearly tripled for preteen girls.

01:30:18.730 --> 01:30:21.330
So Gen Z is in big, big trouble.

01:30:21.330 --> 01:30:22.760
They're hurting.

01:30:22.760 --> 01:30:24.190
They're fragile.

01:30:24.190 --> 01:30:26.020
They're not doing well in the workplace.

01:30:26.020 --> 01:30:28.179
Managers are finding them very hard to work
with.

01:30:28.179 --> 01:30:32.400
So we have a generation that's running off
the rails, and this is not a moralistic thing

01:30:32.400 --> 01:30:33.680
like, "Oh, those kids these days."

01:30:33.680 --> 01:30:34.960
This is a compassionate thing.

01:30:34.960 --> 01:30:35.960
These are our children.

01:30:35.960 --> 01:30:39.310
My kids are 13 and 16.

01:30:39.310 --> 01:30:44.170
Everyone either has kids or that has nieces
and nephews.

01:30:44.170 --> 01:30:47.090
So this is, I think, the greatest emergency
we face.

01:30:47.090 --> 01:30:48.630
The greatest health emergency.

01:30:48.630 --> 01:30:50.710
I think for kids, this is much, much bigger
than COVID.

01:30:50.710 --> 01:30:53.070
COVID was a big deal for old people.

01:30:53.070 --> 01:30:57.291
It wasn't a big deal for children in terms
of the risk, but this is a doubling, more

01:30:57.291 --> 01:31:00.770
than doubling of suicide rates for kids since
2010.

01:31:00.770 --> 01:31:02.050
So that's what I'm working on now.

01:31:02.050 --> 01:31:10.080
Tim Ferriss: What decisions have you made
in your parenting that you feel have been

01:31:10.080 --> 01:31:17.580
perhaps most impactful, less typical, or the
Venn diagram of those two overlapping?

01:31:17.580 --> 01:31:23.139
Jonathan Haidt: The terrible mental health
of Gen Z is caused by two factors.

01:31:23.139 --> 01:31:25.389
One is the vast overprotection.

01:31:25.389 --> 01:31:29.989
Kids need to practice independence, self-governance
from the time they're seven or eight.

01:31:29.989 --> 01:31:33.040
They need independence, but they don't get
it anymore.

01:31:33.040 --> 01:31:35.890
You and I, when we were growing up — you're
younger than me, but I presume you were allowed

01:31:35.890 --> 01:31:37.159
to roam around your neighborhood when you
were eight years old.

01:31:37.159 --> 01:31:38.219
Tim Ferriss: Oh, I was free range.

01:31:38.219 --> 01:31:39.219
Yeah.

01:31:39.219 --> 01:31:40.810
Riding bikes everywhere when I was younger.

01:31:40.810 --> 01:31:41.960
I was also in a rural environment.

01:31:41.960 --> 01:31:43.980
But yeah, I was out and about very early.

01:31:43.980 --> 01:31:44.980
Jonathan Haidt: Yeah.

01:31:44.980 --> 01:31:46.659
So kids must have free-range childhoods.

01:31:46.659 --> 01:31:48.960
They must practice independence.

01:31:48.960 --> 01:31:51.770
And they had that before the '90s.

01:31:51.770 --> 01:31:53.820
And in the '90s, things got incredibly safe.

01:31:53.820 --> 01:31:54.820
We locked up the drunk drivers.

01:31:54.820 --> 01:31:58.750
We took the perverts off the street, crime
plummets, but we freaked out about child induction

01:31:58.750 --> 01:31:59.750
unnecessarily.

01:31:59.750 --> 01:32:04.060
So in the '90s, we stopped letting kids out,
and this affects Gen Z and the late Millennials.

01:32:04.060 --> 01:32:06.370
Anyway, but you asked about me.

01:32:06.370 --> 01:32:12.620
So what my wife and I did, we live here in
New York City, in Greenwich Village, because

01:32:12.620 --> 01:32:16.680
we're friends with Lenore Skenazy, who wrote
this fantastic book, Free-Range Kids.

01:32:16.680 --> 01:32:19.940
We let our kids out to play in Washington
Square Park.

01:32:19.940 --> 01:32:23.950
We sent them out on errands when they were
eight, nine years old.

01:32:23.950 --> 01:32:26.310
New York City was very safe back then.

01:32:26.310 --> 01:32:29.810
It's a little more dangerous now, but it was
very, very safe in the mid — in the 2010s.

01:32:29.810 --> 01:32:34.290
And we sent them out, and we had them walk
to school way — a year or two before anyone

01:32:34.290 --> 01:32:35.330
else was doing it.

01:32:35.330 --> 01:32:38.080
So I'm very glad we did that.

01:32:38.080 --> 01:32:42.350
We also made it clear no social media, at
least until high school, absolutely none in

01:32:42.350 --> 01:32:44.750
middle school.

01:32:44.750 --> 01:32:45.790
And that's been very good.

01:32:45.790 --> 01:32:49.350
When they start sixth grade, my kids tell
me everyone's on Instagram, and we said, "No,

01:32:49.350 --> 01:32:52.580
we are not going to let you do it."

01:32:52.580 --> 01:32:57.860
The one mistake I made was that when my son
wanted Fortnite in sixth grade, and we said

01:32:57.860 --> 01:33:02.190
no because video games can be addictive.

01:33:02.190 --> 01:33:06.360
But now that I've dug into the literature
a lot more, now I see that, yes, a lot of

01:33:06.360 --> 01:33:09.800
boys do get in trouble from video games because
they're on it so much they, they're addicted

01:33:09.800 --> 01:33:11.880
to it, and it pushes out everything else.

01:33:11.880 --> 01:33:18.060
But a couple of hours a day for boys to be
in a group that's battling other boys, it

01:33:18.060 --> 01:33:23.270
turns out that's actually a good thing, and
my son was somewhat cut off in sixth grade.

01:33:23.270 --> 01:33:24.270
Tim Ferriss: Quick question.

01:33:24.270 --> 01:33:26.640
Good, in what respects, how is that assessed?

01:33:26.640 --> 01:33:31.810
Just in terms of acting as a release valve
for aggression or social cohesion?

01:33:31.810 --> 01:33:32.810
Jonathan Haidt: Yeah.

01:33:32.810 --> 01:33:33.810
Yeah.

01:33:33.810 --> 01:33:34.810
Group dynamics.

01:33:34.810 --> 01:33:37.989
So, the release valve idea from Freud does
not end up being true.

01:33:37.989 --> 01:33:39.200
Kids don't need to blow off aggression.

01:33:39.200 --> 01:33:40.930
It's not like that.

01:33:40.930 --> 01:33:45.850
It's rather that girls and boys each need
to practice their gendered behaviors.

01:33:45.850 --> 01:33:50.210
This is what play is, it's play for all mammals,
is the way you practice in childhood the skills

01:33:50.210 --> 01:33:51.739
you'll need as an adult.

01:33:51.739 --> 01:33:53.389
And so boys and girls have very different
play.

01:33:53.389 --> 01:33:57.929
Boys tend to break up into groups to compete
with other groups, and multiplayer video games

01:33:57.929 --> 01:33:59.370
allow them to do that.

01:33:59.370 --> 01:34:00.860
I'm not saying these are great.

01:34:00.860 --> 01:34:05.060
I'm saying, ideally, the kid should be out
having adventures, but given that all the

01:34:05.060 --> 01:34:08.980
kids are home, they're not allowed out until
they're 11, 12 years old.

01:34:08.980 --> 01:34:11.970
So, at least a multiplayer video game allows
them to be part of a group.

01:34:11.970 --> 01:34:13.580
Now, it's not a very creative group.

01:34:13.580 --> 01:34:15.600
The rules are all set by the company.

01:34:15.600 --> 01:34:19.100
It's not like video games are anywhere near
as good as being out on their own, but they're

01:34:19.100 --> 01:34:24.159
not bad until it gets to be heavy.

01:34:24.159 --> 01:34:25.520
That was a small mistake.

01:34:25.520 --> 01:34:29.220
Once COVID hit, we did let him get Fortnite,
and then that was the only way he talked with

01:34:29.220 --> 01:34:30.220
his friends.

01:34:30.220 --> 01:34:32.210
So, that was okay.

01:34:32.210 --> 01:34:36.630
The one mistake I think we did make was we
tried a few different summer camps.

01:34:36.630 --> 01:34:41.430
We never quite found one, but I wish we'd
found a really good summer — and I would

01:34:41.430 --> 01:34:43.420
urge this for everybody who's a parent of
young kids.

01:34:43.420 --> 01:34:48.470
From the time your kid is about eight or nine,
certainly I'd say eight, find a sleep-away

01:34:48.470 --> 01:34:51.810
camp that is pretty unstructured and unsupervised.

01:34:51.810 --> 01:34:54.449
The kids have to have a lot of independence.

01:34:54.449 --> 01:34:56.540
Some summer camps now are so over-protective.

01:34:56.540 --> 01:34:59.020
You can't go to the bathroom unless you have
an escort.

01:34:59.020 --> 01:35:02.040
And sometimes you need two escorts because
what if one escort falls down and gets hurt?

01:35:02.040 --> 01:35:03.620
It's crazy, crazy overprotective everywhere.

01:35:03.620 --> 01:35:08.489
But if you can find a summer camp that is
not crazy overprotective, send your kid there

01:35:08.489 --> 01:35:09.590
every summer.

01:35:09.590 --> 01:35:14.790
That, I think, is one of the few chances they
really have to develop skills to be out in

01:35:14.790 --> 01:35:15.790
the woods.

01:35:15.790 --> 01:35:17.250
So, I wish we'd done that.

01:35:17.250 --> 01:35:19.800
Tim Ferriss: I was fortunate in that respect.

01:35:19.800 --> 01:35:28.710
I got sent off to summer camps in Wyoming
where we would work on ranches, got kicked

01:35:28.710 --> 01:35:29.710
by horses.

01:35:29.710 --> 01:35:36.860
And I mean, I didn't suffer any terrible injuries,
but it wasn't free of injuries, but that was

01:35:36.860 --> 01:35:37.860
okay.

01:35:37.860 --> 01:35:39.560
Nothing was cataclysmic.

01:35:39.560 --> 01:35:44.730
And that, I think, combined — I mean, I
don't have kids, but if I do, and I hope I

01:35:44.730 --> 01:35:53.350
do at some point, I also think a year abroad
in a critical window similar to mine.

01:35:53.350 --> 01:36:00.909
Because of that forced independence, and there
are other reasons, there are other compelling

01:36:00.909 --> 01:36:01.909
reasons.

01:36:01.909 --> 01:36:07.700
But I came back a fundamentally different
person in terms of my confidence in my abilities

01:36:07.700 --> 01:36:10.830
to handle uncertainty and to adapt.

01:36:10.830 --> 01:36:13.350
It was just night and day.

01:36:13.350 --> 01:36:14.770
Complete face shift.

01:36:14.770 --> 01:36:15.770
Jonathan Haidt: That's right.

01:36:15.770 --> 01:36:17.180
That's how kids learn to manage risk for themselves.

01:36:17.180 --> 01:36:18.369
You learn to be a risk taker.

01:36:18.369 --> 01:36:20.739
When we overprotect kids, we're not doing
them any favors.

01:36:20.739 --> 01:36:22.350
We're not keeping them safe.

01:36:22.350 --> 01:36:26.890
We're increasing their odds of anxiety and
depression, self-harm and suicide.

01:36:26.890 --> 01:36:29.510
And we're depriving them of the ability to
judge risks for themselves.

01:36:29.510 --> 01:36:32.239
They're going to be less successful, less
likely to start businesses.

01:36:32.239 --> 01:36:33.920
No, we've really messed up Gen Z.

01:36:33.920 --> 01:36:42.290
Tim Ferriss: I feel like one other unexpected
blessing in my life was transitioning from

01:36:42.290 --> 01:36:47.150
a very bad public school on Long Island to
a very good boarding school in New Hampshire,

01:36:47.150 --> 01:36:55.560
which was actually largely my idea, encouraged
by teachers, a handful of good teachers, at

01:36:55.560 --> 01:37:02.530
the pretty low quality public school on Long
Island, who effectively said, "You should

01:37:02.530 --> 01:37:04.280
try to get out of here."

01:37:04.280 --> 01:37:07.270
So I do owe them thanks.

01:37:07.270 --> 01:37:11.460
When I went to the boarding school, which
was very similar to, let's say, a Dead Poets

01:37:11.460 --> 01:37:19.190
Society type of setup, but classes six days
a week, mandatory sports.

01:37:19.190 --> 01:37:27.780
And looking back, the mandatory sports, I
think, was the force multiplier that allowed

01:37:27.780 --> 01:37:33.030
everything else to go more smoothly on so
many levels.

01:37:33.030 --> 01:37:38.330
If I get to the point, and I hope I do, when
I'm parenting, I think physical activity in

01:37:38.330 --> 01:37:43.489
group sports will probably play a compelling
role.

01:37:43.489 --> 01:37:46.050
I don't know if there's any research to support
that, but —

01:37:46.050 --> 01:37:47.050
Jonathan Haidt: Oh, there is.

01:37:47.050 --> 01:37:48.050
There is.

01:37:48.050 --> 01:37:49.050
But just two points.

01:37:49.050 --> 01:37:51.199
I think you're basically right, but just two
points of nuance.

01:37:51.199 --> 01:37:55.000
One is to the extent that it's heavily supervised
and there's a coach, it's less nutritious.

01:37:55.000 --> 01:37:56.840
They're not going to learn as much.

01:37:56.840 --> 01:38:02.369
The ideal is for kids to be out making up
their own games, enforcing the rules, because

01:38:02.369 --> 01:38:04.810
then you have to keep the game going.

01:38:04.810 --> 01:38:07.310
You have to learn how to adapt to other kids.

01:38:07.310 --> 01:38:11.270
Whereas if there's a coach or a referee, then
what you learn is, "How do I get the referee

01:38:11.270 --> 01:38:12.790
to come in on my side?"

01:38:12.790 --> 01:38:16.230
And that's good preparation for college today
where if somebody says something, how do I

01:38:16.230 --> 01:38:17.900
get a dean to punish him?

01:38:17.900 --> 01:38:19.670
But that's bad preparation for life.

01:38:19.670 --> 01:38:21.150
That's the first thing.

01:38:21.150 --> 01:38:27.230
Second thing, so I just came across a finding
that participation in individualist sports,

01:38:27.230 --> 01:38:30.030
like gymnastics and especially ballet, is
bad.

01:38:30.030 --> 01:38:32.400
It actually is conducive to bad mental health.

01:38:32.400 --> 01:38:35.750
I would never let my daughter do ballet.

01:38:35.750 --> 01:38:40.770
But team sports where you're — soccer, basketball,
where you're really functioning as a team

01:38:40.770 --> 01:38:44.170
over and over again, those have very good
effects on mental health and development.

01:38:44.170 --> 01:38:45.570
So, I would agree with that.

01:38:45.570 --> 01:38:46.910
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:38:46.910 --> 01:38:54.210
My sport was wrestling, which was certainly
an individual sport, practiced in a team context.

01:38:54.210 --> 01:38:58.980
You would have wrestling partners and so on.

01:38:58.980 --> 01:39:04.500
I would have a child do wrestling, I think.

01:39:04.500 --> 01:39:08.750
I'd have them wear headgear because I don't
think cauliflower ears work terribly well

01:39:08.750 --> 01:39:10.050
for most people.

01:39:10.050 --> 01:39:13.429
It goes doubly for females, I would say.

01:39:13.429 --> 01:39:17.680
And if you haven't seen cauliflower on female
ears, I mean, it is scar tissue that will

01:39:17.680 --> 01:39:20.370
not go away unless surgically removed.

01:39:20.370 --> 01:39:25.930
Saw a lot of it in Japan in Judo players,
male and female, lots of cauliflowers.

01:39:25.930 --> 01:39:35.810
But I found it to be, for me personally, at
least, an excellent venue for proving to myself

01:39:35.810 --> 01:39:38.520
that I could exceed the limitations I thought
I had.

01:39:38.520 --> 01:39:42.540
And in terms of work capacity and pain tolerance,
also.

01:39:42.540 --> 01:39:45.080
I will say critical, critical components.

01:39:45.080 --> 01:39:50.640
So I can't totally, with any conviction, say
that it was wrestling that did this, although

01:39:50.640 --> 01:39:53.770
I have some belief that it contributed.

01:39:53.770 --> 01:40:01.560
The coach, John Buxton, changed the lives
of almost every athlete he coached.

01:40:01.560 --> 01:40:04.400
And to this day, I'm still close to him.

01:40:04.400 --> 01:40:09.750
And many people in that wrestling team went
on to do some pretty outstanding things.

01:40:09.750 --> 01:40:16.350
And they have all, to my knowledge, just about
all of them, have mentioned Mr. Buxton.

01:40:16.350 --> 01:40:18.310
We have a tough time calling him —
Jonathan Haidt: I have to ask you a question

01:40:18.310 --> 01:40:19.310
then.

01:40:19.310 --> 01:40:20.310
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:40:20.310 --> 01:40:21.310
Jonathan Haidt: A question.

01:40:21.310 --> 01:40:22.790
Did he have a tough coaching style or did
he have a warm, nurturing style?

01:40:22.790 --> 01:40:24.790
Tim Ferriss: He was extremely, extremely tough.

01:40:24.790 --> 01:40:25.929
And this is what I was going to bring up.

01:40:25.929 --> 01:40:27.860
He was extremely tough.

01:40:27.860 --> 01:40:31.630
He did not suffer fools gladly at all.

01:40:31.630 --> 01:40:37.360
If people complained about something, they
could fix themselves or should try to fix

01:40:37.360 --> 01:40:40.290
themselves, he did not tolerate it.

01:40:40.290 --> 01:40:44.360
He was a very tough coach, and he was a very,
very —

01:40:44.360 --> 01:40:45.360
Jonathan Haidt: That's what I expected.

01:40:45.360 --> 01:40:47.970
Tim Ferriss: And he was a very, very, very
good performer.

01:40:47.970 --> 01:40:53.470
So, he himself had been an outstanding wrestler
and could go toe to toe with anyone in the

01:40:53.470 --> 01:40:57.710
group, including people who went on to become
All Americans.

01:40:57.710 --> 01:41:04.540
I mean, granted, in their particular corner
of the wrestling world and — exceptionally

01:41:04.540 --> 01:41:05.540
tough.

01:41:05.540 --> 01:41:10.369
And he would give occasional positive feedback.

01:41:10.369 --> 01:41:13.770
And I don't want to say he was stingy, but
you've really had to earn it.

01:41:13.770 --> 01:41:15.040
You really, really had to earn it.

01:41:15.040 --> 01:41:18.929
Jonathan Haidt: Yes, that's what I figured
because wrestling is probably the best antifragile

01:41:18.929 --> 01:41:21.929
sports there is, that is more than any other
sport.

01:41:21.929 --> 01:41:25.290
Wrestling is really going to be about developing
your antifragility, your strength in the face

01:41:25.290 --> 01:41:27.250
of adversity, testing your strength.

01:41:27.250 --> 01:41:30.580
So the sport itself is very conducive to growth
in that way.

01:41:30.580 --> 01:41:33.199
And I kind of knew, given how grateful you
were, I kind of knew that he was going to

01:41:33.199 --> 01:41:36.900
be a tough coach, not a warm, nurturing coach.

01:41:36.900 --> 01:41:41.790
Because you really need, to get the full benefit
of the growth into strength, you need someone

01:41:41.790 --> 01:41:42.790
to push you.

01:41:42.790 --> 01:41:44.040
You need someone with high standards.

01:41:44.040 --> 01:41:45.550
You need someone to enforce those standards.

01:41:45.550 --> 01:41:47.119
You need to strive for them.

01:41:47.119 --> 01:41:50.590
A few years ago, when I gave a talk on The
Coddling of the American Mind, I was sitting

01:41:50.590 --> 01:41:56.390
next to somebody who'd been, I think, the
head of the NCAA or high up in it, and this

01:41:56.390 --> 01:41:58.770
was like 2018, 2019.

01:41:58.770 --> 01:42:05.270
He said, "The tough coaching style — it's
not that it's banned, but that coaches are

01:42:05.270 --> 01:42:09.440
fined and they can't use the tough style anymore
because the kids will cry, they'll report

01:42:09.440 --> 01:42:11.150
them, they'll say, 'No, you need to be nurturing.'

01:42:11.150 --> 01:42:15.790
And so tough coaching, I think, is going out
because what if you hurt a kid's feelings?"

01:42:15.790 --> 01:42:20.000
And so in so many ways, we've accepted, and
that's a great untruth, number one, of The

01:42:20.000 --> 01:42:22.310
Coddling of the American Mind is what doesn't
kill you makes you weaker.

01:42:22.310 --> 01:42:23.449
Now, that's wrong.

01:42:23.449 --> 01:42:24.960
That's a falsehood.

01:42:24.960 --> 01:42:27.350
But that's what many people now believe.

01:42:27.350 --> 01:42:30.310
And I think your experience really shows that
no, what doesn't kill you makes you stronger.

01:42:30.310 --> 01:42:34.000
That's what you just said, and that's what
the psychology supports.

01:42:34.000 --> 01:42:38.900
I think the lesson here for parents and teachers
is we need to have standards.

01:42:38.900 --> 01:42:41.460
We need to push kids to meet the standards.

01:42:41.460 --> 01:42:43.170
Sometimes the kids are not going to be happy.

01:42:43.170 --> 01:42:47.600
And as long as we can be reported for pushing
the kids, we're not going to push the kids.

01:42:47.600 --> 01:42:49.590
And the whole generation goes down the tubes.

01:42:49.590 --> 01:42:51.010
So, that's what we're doing.

01:42:51.010 --> 01:42:55.810
Tim Ferriss: And Coach Buxton also, I think,
fundamentally believed — I'll speak for

01:42:55.810 --> 01:43:01.500
myself personally, I don't want to speak for
other folks, but he would reinforce the belief

01:43:01.500 --> 01:43:08.199
that he saw in me the capacity to do more,
to perform better.

01:43:08.199 --> 01:43:15.580
And it was in that context that being a tough
coach was the nice thing to do.

01:43:15.580 --> 01:43:16.580
Does that make sense?

01:43:16.580 --> 01:43:17.580
Jonathan Haidt: Yeah, absolutely.

01:43:17.580 --> 01:43:20.600
Tim Ferriss: In service of realizing absolutely
that unrealized potential.

01:43:20.600 --> 01:43:26.349
And I think all of the athletes he coached
felt that way, that he saw something in us

01:43:26.349 --> 01:43:28.330
that we couldn't easily see.

01:43:28.330 --> 01:43:32.620
And he was pushing us to unlock that potential.

01:43:32.620 --> 01:43:39.889
And it was not abusive for the sake of, say,
breaking someone down, like perhaps a drill

01:43:39.889 --> 01:43:43.909
sergeant might serve the function of shared
privation and that type of environment.

01:43:43.909 --> 01:43:47.640
I think there's a purpose to that, and I think
it actually can be good for the collective.

01:43:47.640 --> 01:43:57.550
But in this particular wrestling context,
the wellspring of the toughness came from

01:43:57.550 --> 01:44:02.070
a place of, in some cases, explicitly saying,
"You can do better.

01:44:02.070 --> 01:44:04.260
And I know you can do better."

01:44:04.260 --> 01:44:05.260
And that was powerful.

01:44:05.260 --> 01:44:06.260
It's very powerful.

01:44:06.260 --> 01:44:07.260
Jonathan Haidt: And that is beautiful.

01:44:07.260 --> 01:44:11.429
And I think what you've just done there is
you've stated what the telos of teaching should

01:44:11.429 --> 01:44:12.429
be.

01:44:12.429 --> 01:44:14.090
Telos is this Greek word that I'm very fond
of nowadays.

01:44:14.090 --> 01:44:17.590
It means the end or purpose for which something
is done.

01:44:17.590 --> 01:44:19.179
The telos of medicine is health.

01:44:19.179 --> 01:44:21.390
What is the telos of a university?

01:44:21.390 --> 01:44:26.520
And I've been focusing on the research aspect
where our telos is truth, but we're also teachers.

01:44:26.520 --> 01:44:29.110
And our telos as teachers is not truth.

01:44:29.110 --> 01:44:32.160
And I've been saying, "Oh, it's the education
of our kids.

01:44:32.160 --> 01:44:33.560
It's learning, teaching."

01:44:33.560 --> 01:44:36.020
But I think what you said is actually better.

01:44:36.020 --> 01:44:42.159
The telos of a teacher is doing this service
of realizing their untapped potential.

01:44:42.159 --> 01:44:43.159
That's what we're trying to do.

01:44:43.159 --> 01:44:44.159
That's what we should be trying to do.

01:44:44.159 --> 01:44:45.159
And we don't always do it.

01:44:45.159 --> 01:44:49.900
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I've been very fortunate
in that respect to have had a handful of teachers

01:44:49.900 --> 01:44:58.389
who were unrelenting in their pushing towards
the truth that they saw, which was their students

01:44:58.389 --> 01:45:01.349
could in fact do the hard things.

01:45:01.349 --> 01:45:02.840
And that was true.

01:45:02.840 --> 01:45:10.470
I'll give thanks to another teacher, professor
Shimano, who was my first Japanese teacher.

01:45:10.470 --> 01:45:16.270
And surprise, surprise, when I transferred
from Spanish to Japanese, I found Japanese

01:45:16.270 --> 01:45:19.600
to be extremely difficult.

01:45:19.600 --> 01:45:24.560
And I almost quit, I want to say, twice.

01:45:24.560 --> 01:45:28.400
Once, just because of the difficulty with
the language grammatically.

01:45:28.400 --> 01:45:33.960
And the second time when we started delving
into the various writing systems in Japanese,

01:45:33.960 --> 01:45:41.910
and I was talked off the ledge by Professor
Shimano, who was able to just say, "Give it

01:45:41.910 --> 01:45:42.910
some time.

01:45:42.910 --> 01:45:44.980
I think you can do this.

01:45:44.980 --> 01:45:48.909
And I'm betting that I can help you do this."

01:45:48.909 --> 01:45:56.639
And good God, if I had not listened to that,
if I had pushed back, if I had become offended

01:45:56.639 --> 01:46:03.639
and walked from that class, my life would
not be what it is today in any respect.

01:46:03.639 --> 01:46:07.020
I feel very, very grateful.

01:46:07.020 --> 01:46:13.989
Let's talk just a little bit, if there's anything
that you would care to add about intellectual

01:46:13.989 --> 01:46:14.989
antifragility.

01:46:14.989 --> 01:46:21.119
Are there other things that people can do,
other practices they can experiment with,

01:46:21.119 --> 01:46:26.739
other resources that you would suggest they
look to develop intellectual antifragility?

01:46:26.739 --> 01:46:28.400
Jonathan Haidt: Yes.

01:46:28.400 --> 01:46:34.389
So, I created a resource, an online resource
with Caroline Mehl, who was working with me

01:46:34.389 --> 01:46:37.970
originally at Heterodox Academy, but it branched
out.

01:46:37.970 --> 01:46:45.750
If listeners go to ConstructiveDialogue.org,
we've created a program called Perspectives,

01:46:45.750 --> 01:46:49.780
that kind of walks you through some of the
moral psychology, "Why are we like this?

01:46:49.780 --> 01:46:50.930
Why are we so tribal?

01:46:50.930 --> 01:46:52.600
Why do we have confirmation bias?"

01:46:52.600 --> 01:46:57.420
And it teaches you it how to open your mind
and open your heart.

01:46:57.420 --> 01:47:03.429
And it teaches you how to engage with people
and how to learn from them, how to listen.

01:47:03.429 --> 01:47:04.500
All the things we've been talking about.

01:47:04.500 --> 01:47:09.440
If you go to Constructive Dialogue, and yes,
dialogue is D-I-A-L-O-G-U-E.org.

01:47:09.440 --> 01:47:13.030
We have a program that we created called Perspectives.

01:47:13.030 --> 01:47:15.849
It used to be called Open Mind.

01:47:15.849 --> 01:47:23.170
And if listeners go there, it was designed
for use in college courses, especially at

01:47:23.170 --> 01:47:27.601
orientation, because if everyone in a group
has these concepts and they use these tools,

01:47:27.601 --> 01:47:28.601
you're going to have great discussions.

01:47:28.601 --> 01:47:31.290
You're going to be able to talk about hard
things.

01:47:31.290 --> 01:47:33.530
It works really well in businesses as well.

01:47:33.530 --> 01:47:37.310
So, that's one resource.

01:47:37.310 --> 01:47:41.409
Another resource, I think, what I found really
helped me when I was writing The Righteous

01:47:41.409 --> 01:47:45.330
Mind was I just subscribed to the best writing
on the other side.

01:47:45.330 --> 01:47:50.120
I was very much on the Left when I started
the book, and I would read The New Republic

01:47:50.120 --> 01:47:53.930
and The New York Times or things I read back
when The New Republic was a good magazine.

01:47:53.930 --> 01:47:59.210
But I subscribed to National Review and The
Wall Street Journal.

01:47:59.210 --> 01:48:04.670
And National Review has all these really smart
Center Right intellectuals, great writing.

01:48:04.670 --> 01:48:08.750
And I would read about current events on the
Left and the Right.

01:48:08.750 --> 01:48:12.060
I just understood things much better.

01:48:12.060 --> 01:48:15.940
It's kind of trite, but you literally can't
understand something from looking at a single

01:48:15.940 --> 01:48:16.940
perspective.

01:48:16.940 --> 01:48:19.320
You kind of have to walk around it and look
at it from all sides.

01:48:19.320 --> 01:48:25.510
So, resources are out there, and the internet
is such that it will guide you to fantastic

01:48:25.510 --> 01:48:26.840
resources from each perspective.

01:48:26.840 --> 01:48:31.630
And if you don't seek them out, then what
you get, if you're on the Left, you do encounter

01:48:31.630 --> 01:48:37.750
some Conservative ideas, but usually you encounter
reports of them in their worst form or reports

01:48:37.750 --> 01:48:40.270
to them being held up to ridicule.

01:48:40.270 --> 01:48:42.690
And same thing if you're on the right.

01:48:42.690 --> 01:48:49.710
Yeah, if you want to be smarter and stronger,
you must seek out different perspectives.

01:48:49.710 --> 01:48:51.480
It's kind of trite, but there it is.

01:48:51.480 --> 01:48:53.130
Tim Ferriss: So, ConstructiveDialogue.org.

01:48:53.130 --> 01:48:55.290
We'll link to all of these in the show notes.

01:48:55.290 --> 01:49:00.840
And I'll also create a URL by the time this
comes up, it'll work, which will just be tim.blog/haidt,

01:49:00.840 --> 01:49:08.110
H-A-I-D-T, and that will forward to this episode,
so people can find all the links in the show

01:49:08.110 --> 01:49:11.540
notes really easily.

01:49:11.540 --> 01:49:13.000
Let me ask you the billboard question.

01:49:13.000 --> 01:49:15.730
I'm curious what your take on this might be.

01:49:15.730 --> 01:49:21.290
The billboard question is sometimes a dead
end, and I'll take full blame for that if

01:49:21.290 --> 01:49:22.290
it is.

01:49:22.290 --> 01:49:30.750
But if you could put a message, a quote, anything
at all on a billboard, metaphorically speaking,

01:49:30.750 --> 01:49:35.380
to get something out to billions of people,
assuming they would all understand it, what

01:49:35.380 --> 01:49:36.909
might you put on that billboard?

01:49:36.909 --> 01:49:39.690
Jonathan Haidt: Ah, that's actually easy.

01:49:39.690 --> 01:49:41.560
Actually, I'm going to nominate two.

01:49:41.560 --> 01:49:42.910
If you'll give me the front and the back of
the billboard.

01:49:42.910 --> 01:49:43.910
Tim Ferriss: I'll give you two.

01:49:43.910 --> 01:49:44.969
Jonathan Haidt: Okay.

01:49:44.969 --> 01:49:51.310
The first is a quote from a Zen Buddhist philosopher
in the eighth century, Seng-ts'an was his

01:49:51.310 --> 01:49:53.580
name, Chinese Buddhist philosopher.

01:49:53.580 --> 01:49:55.810
And I've used this quote in most of my books.

01:49:55.810 --> 01:49:56.810
I'll just read the whole thing.

01:49:56.810 --> 01:49:59.600
He says,
"The Perfect Way is only difficult for those

01:49:59.600 --> 01:50:04.040
who pick and choose;
Do not like, do not dislike; all will then

01:50:04.040 --> 01:50:05.730
be clear.

01:50:05.730 --> 01:50:09.870
Make a hairbreadth difference, and Heaven
and Earth are set apart;

01:50:09.870 --> 01:50:14.840
If you want the truth to stand clear before
you, never be for or against.

01:50:14.840 --> 01:50:20.860
The struggle between 'for' and 'against' is
the mind's worst disease."

01:50:20.860 --> 01:50:25.540
This is what we were talking about before,
about getting beyond moralism.

01:50:25.540 --> 01:50:28.770
When you're moralistic, when you're judging,
when you're saying, "This is good, this is

01:50:28.770 --> 01:50:31.040
bad," you get stupider.

01:50:31.040 --> 01:50:34.150
You don't understand the nuance of things.

01:50:34.150 --> 01:50:39.230
This quote is about sort of demoralization,
not like being demoralized, but sucking the

01:50:39.230 --> 01:50:43.940
moralism or the morality out of something
and just trying to understand it.

01:50:43.940 --> 01:50:45.980
That's the first.

01:50:45.980 --> 01:50:51.130
And then if I have the back of the billboard,
I would put this quote from Joseph Campbell.

01:50:51.130 --> 01:50:56.190
So, Joseph Campbell was a professor at, I
think, it was Bryn Mawr or Sarah Lawrence

01:50:56.190 --> 01:50:59.480
rather, in the late 20th century.

01:50:59.480 --> 01:51:00.579
He studied myths.

01:51:00.579 --> 01:51:05.150
And there was a wonderful PBS series on, I
think, The Hero with a Thousand Faces was

01:51:05.150 --> 01:51:06.429
one of his books.

01:51:06.429 --> 01:51:13.290
And so Campbell, this motto, this really calms
me because I'm very alarmed at the way things

01:51:13.290 --> 01:51:17.480
are going, the way our complex dynamical system
is kind of veering into chaos.

01:51:17.480 --> 01:51:21.580
And I found this quote to be really, really
comforting, and I sort of have it up on my

01:51:21.580 --> 01:51:23.980
walls, various places.

01:51:23.980 --> 01:51:30.480
He says the lesson from his studying of heroes,
myths, heroes journey stories around the world

01:51:30.480 --> 01:51:35.690
is this, "Participate joyfully in the sorrows
of the world.

01:51:35.690 --> 01:51:40.230
We cannot cure the world of sorrows, but we
can choose to live in joy.

01:51:40.230 --> 01:51:46.430
The warrior's approach is to say yes to life,
yes to it all."

01:51:46.430 --> 01:51:47.800
That's what I'd put up.

01:51:47.800 --> 01:51:49.119
Tim Ferriss: Love it.

01:51:49.119 --> 01:51:51.500
That's a great combination.

01:51:51.500 --> 01:51:56.329
And you mentioned Buddhism and Stoicism earlier.

01:51:56.329 --> 01:52:05.060
I don't know if you feel this way, but I have
felt over time that there is tremendous overlap

01:52:05.060 --> 01:52:11.920
in those two, let's just call it lineages,
or broadly speaking schools of thought.

01:52:11.920 --> 01:52:21.619
And I suppose both are also related to your
front and back of the billboard in the sense

01:52:21.619 --> 01:52:32.310
of cultivating equanimity, a sense of being
unperturbed by the vicissitudes, if I could

01:52:32.310 --> 01:52:41.099
use a fancy word, of life, the daily flux,
all of these tiny and large tragedies that

01:52:41.099 --> 01:52:44.530
befall everyone at various points.

01:52:44.530 --> 01:52:54.070
How do you revisit some of these practical
philosophies for yourself?

01:52:54.070 --> 01:52:58.739
Let's just say that you're going through a
period or there's an event where you start

01:52:58.739 --> 01:53:03.540
to feel dysregulated, consumed by emotions,
what do you do?

01:53:03.540 --> 01:53:05.050
Jonathan Haidt: Oh, yeah.

01:53:05.050 --> 01:53:06.150
Thank you for that.

01:53:06.150 --> 01:53:10.020
Earlier you asked me, do I have practices?

01:53:10.020 --> 01:53:16.159
There was the summer of 2017, I think it was,
when Trump was threatening nuclear war against

01:53:16.159 --> 01:53:21.700
— there was a possibility of nuclear war
with North Korea.

01:53:21.700 --> 01:53:25.159
And I really thought, "I think he might actually
want to do it."

01:53:25.159 --> 01:53:29.469
And I was really thinking, "This really could
be the end of the world."

01:53:29.469 --> 01:53:33.480
And it was then that I started rereading Stoic
writings in the morning.

01:53:33.480 --> 01:53:39.540
And my favorite two are Marcus Aurelius' Meditations,
the Gregory Hays translation is, I think,

01:53:39.540 --> 01:53:40.670
the best one.

01:53:40.670 --> 01:53:43.820
And then also Epictetus, his collected works.

01:53:43.820 --> 01:53:47.679
And what I do whenever I read, I do all my
reading electronically now, because I like

01:53:47.679 --> 01:53:48.849
to copy out text.

01:53:48.849 --> 01:53:53.790
So I copy my favorite lines out into an Evernote
file.

01:53:53.790 --> 01:53:57.579
And so what I do in the mornings, I don't
even have it in my calendar, "Let me see,

01:53:57.579 --> 01:53:58.770
how do I record in my calendar..."

01:53:58.770 --> 01:54:03.360
Every day repeating, there's a couple different
things I do, but it's basically either reading

01:54:03.360 --> 01:54:06.840
Stoicism or Buddhism or reflecting on psychotherapy.

01:54:06.840 --> 01:54:08.930
I've been in therapy for a while.

01:54:08.930 --> 01:54:12.699
So, yes, and I have it right here.

01:54:12.699 --> 01:54:15.489
Stoic or Buddhists reading, and I have to
check it off each morning.

01:54:15.489 --> 01:54:20.179
And I do that before I check email, before
anything else.

01:54:20.179 --> 01:54:21.520
And right, you're right.

01:54:21.520 --> 01:54:23.520
Stoicism and Buddhism, they're very, very
similar.

01:54:23.520 --> 01:54:28.739
Except Stoicism is sort of a Western version
because it is more about action in the world.

01:54:28.739 --> 01:54:32.300
Now, Buddhism, there are Buddhisms that are
very much in the world, there are those that

01:54:32.300 --> 01:54:34.870
pull you away more.

01:54:34.870 --> 01:54:38.710
But there is a sort of an Eastern sensibility
in Daoism about, "Don't do anything, just

01:54:38.710 --> 01:54:39.710
settle.

01:54:39.710 --> 01:54:41.130
Just let the world arrange itself."

01:54:41.130 --> 01:54:45.860
And Westerners are not like that and Stoics
are not like that, but it is like act in the

01:54:45.860 --> 01:54:50.320
world, "Okay, you're the emperor of Rome,
but you're still getting upset about things

01:54:50.320 --> 01:54:52.980
and you have to — everything's crazy around
you."

01:54:52.980 --> 01:54:54.130
But it's not just for emperors.

01:54:54.130 --> 01:54:55.460
Epictetus was a slave.

01:54:55.460 --> 01:54:58.500
And so it actually works at any level of society.

01:54:58.500 --> 01:55:01.500
You can never control everything, even if
you're the emperor.

01:55:01.500 --> 01:55:06.820
Both of them are just incredibly psychologically
wise ways of living in a world that you can't

01:55:06.820 --> 01:55:13.889
control and maintaining not just your equanimity,
but even the capacity to be openhearted and

01:55:13.889 --> 01:55:14.889
loving.

01:55:14.889 --> 01:55:18.940
There's very similar passages not about necessarily
loving your enemies, but actually sometimes

01:55:18.940 --> 01:55:24.409
it is like, and they both use the same technique
of saying, "And remember, you'll both be dead

01:55:24.409 --> 01:55:26.170
before long."

01:55:26.170 --> 01:55:30.740
That's a quote from The Dhammapada, "How can
you quarrel, knowing that you'll both be dead

01:55:30.740 --> 01:55:31.940
before long?"

01:55:31.940 --> 01:55:33.369
It's all trivial.

01:55:33.369 --> 01:55:37.619
Tim Ferriss: How long is your document that
you review in the morning, that Evernote file?

01:55:37.619 --> 01:55:41.310
Jonathan Haidt: Well, I've got a whole big
one for Seneca, who I'm going through Seneca.

01:55:41.310 --> 01:55:46.110
There's just so much writing and it's not
as dense in terms of gems, but I've got one

01:55:46.110 --> 01:55:49.489
for Epictetus, one for Aurelius, one for Buddha.

01:55:49.489 --> 01:55:57.310
You know what, if you go to the coddling.com,
which is the webpage for The Coddling of the

01:55:57.310 --> 01:56:03.180
American Mind, and you go to Solutions, Better
Mental Health, I've actually put up at the

01:56:03.180 --> 01:56:08.520
bottom, I've put up a collection of sayings
by Marcus Aurelius that basically are about

01:56:08.520 --> 01:56:10.619
the three great untruths of the book.

01:56:10.619 --> 01:56:11.969
So, here's one.

01:56:11.969 --> 01:56:18.619
"Today I escaped from anxiety, or no, I discarded
it because it was within me in my own perceptions,

01:56:18.619 --> 01:56:20.690
not outside."

01:56:20.690 --> 01:56:25.190
This is the great untruth that we cover in
the book is always trust your feelings.

01:56:25.190 --> 01:56:30.030
But what the Buddhists and the Stoics and
CBT tell us is, "No, don't always trust your

01:56:30.030 --> 01:56:31.030
feelings.

01:56:31.030 --> 01:56:32.030
Question them.

01:56:32.030 --> 01:56:36.150
Sometimes they're legitimate, but sometimes
they're gross overreactions to nothing."

01:56:36.150 --> 01:56:40.010
Anyway, yeah, those are the great wisdom traditions
in my opinion.

01:56:40.010 --> 01:56:45.000
Tim Ferriss: How long do you spend in the
morning, if you check the box of that calendar

01:56:45.000 --> 01:56:47.030
reminder with that reading?

01:56:47.030 --> 01:56:48.750
Jonathan Haidt: Oh, it's about 10 minutes.

01:56:48.750 --> 01:56:49.750
Tim Ferriss: 10 minutes.

01:56:49.750 --> 01:56:53.632
Jonathan Haidt: Recently, I'm doing a loving-kindness
meditation because I'm very cerebral and I

01:56:53.632 --> 01:56:55.110
actually sort of need to open my heart more.

01:56:55.110 --> 01:56:57.480
But I make sort of a coffee substitute.

01:56:57.480 --> 01:57:01.190
I'm very sensitive to caffeine, so I'm experimenting
with these mushroom-based things.

01:57:01.190 --> 01:57:10.670
Anyway, but I make a hot beverage and then
I sit on a comfortable sofa and just about

01:57:10.670 --> 01:57:15.750
10 minutes, because what I found is that my
MBA students, I asked, "How many of you check

01:57:15.750 --> 01:57:18.389
your phone before you get up to pee?

01:57:18.389 --> 01:57:20.820
And how many of you wait until you actually
have gone into the bathroom?"

01:57:20.820 --> 01:57:21.889
And very few of them wait.

01:57:21.889 --> 01:57:22.969
It's like right away.

01:57:22.969 --> 01:57:24.969
Okay, turn on the stream.

01:57:24.969 --> 01:57:27.210
Turn on the stream, have it come in.

01:57:27.210 --> 01:57:28.429
No, don't do that.

01:57:28.429 --> 01:57:33.630
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, have a better boot up sequence
for your morning and day.

01:57:33.630 --> 01:57:34.650
Jonathan Haidt: Exactly.

01:57:34.650 --> 01:57:35.650
That's right.

01:57:35.650 --> 01:57:37.530
Tim Ferriss: Jon, I really appreciate all
the time.

01:57:37.530 --> 01:57:38.530
This has been a lot of fun.

01:57:38.530 --> 01:57:40.200
I've copious notes.

01:57:40.200 --> 01:57:44.620
There are many things that I'm going to be
looking up, and I think the first will be

01:57:44.620 --> 01:57:46.719
On Liberty Chapter Two.

01:57:46.719 --> 01:57:50.270
I'm going to check in and give that a read.

01:57:50.270 --> 01:57:52.270
And then I have many others.

01:57:52.270 --> 01:57:54.139
Is there anything else that you would like
to say?

01:57:54.139 --> 01:57:59.610
Any closing comments, any questions, anything
you'd like to point my audience to that we

01:57:59.610 --> 01:58:01.570
haven't mentioned or that we have mentioned?

01:58:01.570 --> 01:58:03.989
Any complaints you'd like to lodge?

01:58:03.989 --> 01:58:04.989
Anything at all?

01:58:04.989 --> 01:58:05.989
Jonathan Haidt: Yeah.

01:58:05.989 --> 01:58:11.179
No, I'd just like to say, my mission when
I moved to Stern Business School, everyone

01:58:11.179 --> 01:58:12.270
has a mission statement.

01:58:12.270 --> 01:58:17.219
I made a mission statement, and my mission
is to use my research and that of others in

01:58:17.219 --> 01:58:21.429
moral psychology to help important institutions
work better.

01:58:21.429 --> 01:58:23.590
And I've started various organizations.

01:58:23.590 --> 01:58:29.000
So, if you're concerned about universities,
please go to HeterodoxAcademy.org and join

01:58:29.000 --> 01:58:32.699
if you're an academic or support us financially,
if you can.

01:58:32.699 --> 01:58:37.150
If you're concerned about people's ability
to talk to each other or you need to address

01:58:37.150 --> 01:58:42.940
conflict in your own organization, please
go to ConstructiveDialogue.org.

01:58:42.940 --> 01:58:49.290
If you are a parent and you have kids born
after 1996, you have Gen Z kids, please go

01:58:49.290 --> 01:58:51.540
to LetGrow.org.

01:58:51.540 --> 01:58:56.250
A site I started with Lenore Skenazy to encourage
free range parenting.

01:58:56.250 --> 01:58:58.619
And so, all three of those are nonprofit foundations.

01:58:58.619 --> 01:59:00.030
We really need help.

01:59:00.030 --> 01:59:01.659
I'm trying hard to raise money for them.

01:59:01.659 --> 01:59:02.949
So, please support us.

01:59:02.949 --> 01:59:06.889
And all of them, I hope, will help you solve
problems in your own lives and in your own

01:59:06.889 --> 01:59:09.210
organizations and your own families.

01:59:09.210 --> 01:59:10.290
Tim Ferriss: Perfect.

01:59:10.290 --> 01:59:11.670
That's the perfect place to wrap up.

01:59:11.670 --> 01:59:18.480
And for everyone listening, all of these links
I will gather and put also in the show notes

01:59:18.480 --> 01:59:24.210
so they'll be collected in one place and you
can click them to take yourself to all these

01:59:24.210 --> 01:59:25.210
destinations.

01:59:25.210 --> 01:59:26.210
tim.blog/Haidt, H-A-I-D-T.

01:59:26.210 --> 01:59:36.560
On Twitter, be careful, it's a full contact
sport out there, @JonHaidt, and JonathanHaidt.com.

01:59:36.560 --> 01:59:40.599
Jon, thank you so much for taking the time
and being so game.

01:59:40.599 --> 01:59:41.599
I really appreciate it.

01:59:41.599 --> 01:59:42.599
Jonathan Haidt: Tim, thank you.

01:59:42.599 --> 01:59:44.239
This was rollicking and good fun.

01:59:44.239 --> 01:59:49.210
Tim Ferriss: And to everybody out there, be
just a little kinder than is necessary.

01:59:49.210 --> 01:59:51.320
Don't believe everything that you think.

01:59:51.320 --> 01:59:54.180
Ask questions before you make strong statements.

01:59:54.180 --> 01:59:55.469
And thanks for listening.

